\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Yen rebound supports Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , still set for weekly losses\b0\par\par\uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2014\par 06:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Won to fall 4th week, longest weekly loss since May 2013               \par \par \uc2 * S.Korea \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected; won down vs yen               \par \par \uc2 * Rupiah up 0.4 pct for week on fuel price, rate hikes   (Adds text, updates prices)               \par \par \uc2 By Jongwoo Cheon               \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 21 (Reuters) - Emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on Friday as the yen rebounded after Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso warned against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent swift fall, although most regional units were set for another week of losses.               \par \par \uc2 The yen gained as Aso earlier described the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakening as "too rapid," but ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the slide.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's won rebounded from a near 15-month low as investors covered short positions and on demand from exporters for month-end settlements. The Malaysian ringgit  advanced as traders also cut bearish bets.               \par \par \uc2 Still, emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unlikely to extend gains as the yen remains on a broadly weakening trend, traders and analysts said.               \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pared some of its sharp gains as Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  dissolved parliament's lower house for a snap election on Dec. 14.               \par \par \uc2 "The yen's move today is just a correction as the recent slide was seen excessive. Its depreciation trend remains intact," said Yuna Park, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond analyst at \uc2 Dongbu Securities\uc2  in Seoul.               \par \par \uc2 "Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may rebound a bit, but they are not free from the yen. In addition, the dollar will stay firm too," Park added.               \par \par \uc2 Most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were already poised to post weekly losses, led by the won, as the yen hit a seven-year low against the dollar on Thursday.               \par \par \uc2 The won has lost 1.1 percent against the dollar so far this week, on course for its fourth week of declines, according to Thomson Reuters data. That would be the longest weekly losses since May last year.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the won's strength against the yen  on concerns its exporters will erode their competitiveness against Japanese rivals.               \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar has slid 0.5 percent with the island's exporters also seen vulnerable to weakness in the yen and the won. The ringgit has fallen 0.3 percent.               \par \par \uc2 India's rupee and the Philippine peso  have eased 0.2 percent each.               \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah stood firm in the face of falls in regional peers, having risen 0.4 percent, continuing to benefit from President Joko Widodo long-awaited decision to raise fuel prices and free up funds to rejuvenate Southeast Asia's biggest economy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has been underpinned by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise rate hike, which was aimed at tempering an expected upsurge in inflation following the fuel-hike move.               \par \par \uc2 China's yuan has gained 0.1 percent on expectations of inflows thanks to a new link between the Hong Kong and Shanghai stock exchanges.                              \par \par \uc2 WON               \par \par \uc2 The won pared some of its daily gains as traders sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it rose above 1,110 per dollar with the authorities suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the won lost as much as 0.6 percent to 9.4536. On Thursday, the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a six-year high of 9.3660.               \par \par \uc2 "It is a great chance to lift the yen/won," said a foreign bank trader in Seoul, referring to the yen's rebound on Friday.               \par \par \uc2 RINGGIT               \par \par \uc2 The ringgit rose as investors covered short positions, tracking the yen's rebound.               \par \par \uc2 Still, the upside in the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was limited due to persistent concerns over a slowing economy.               \par \par \uc2 Malaysia's inflation rose 2.8 percent in October from a year earlier due to higher fuel prices after the government cut subsidies, data showed.                              \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       117.69         118.20       +0.43\par  Sing dlr        1.2979         1.2999       +0.15\par  Taiwan dlr      30.920         31.015       +0.31\par  Korean won     1112.20        1115.10       +0.26\par  Baht             32.77          32.81       +0.14\par  Peso             45.02          45.07       +0.12\par  Rupiah        12165.