\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Rupiah hits 8-month low; most Asia  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  set for monthly loss\b0\par\par\uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2014\par 07:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Rupiah touches near 8-month low; leads monthly losses\par     * Most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on track for monthly losses\par     * Fed rate rise expectations buoy dollar vs Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par     By Masayuki Kitano\par     SINGAPORE, Sept 30 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah hit an\par eight-month low on Tuesday and was on track to lead monthly\par losses in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which have taken a hit as investors\par position for an eventual rise in U.S. interest rates.\par     The rupiah fell to as low as 12,230 versus the dollar\par , its lowest since early February.\par     The dollar has rallied about 4 percent versus the rupiah so\par far in September, making the rupiah the worst-performing\par emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this month.\par     The dollar has gained 3.8 percent versus the South Korean\par won and 3.7 percent against the Malaysian ringgit\par  in September. \par     Many Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  edged higher on Tuesday, getting a bit\par of a respite from their recent selloff.  \par     Still, most were on track for monthly losses, having come\par under pressure as investors positioned for the possibility that\par the U.S. Federal Reserve could raise borrowing costs faster than\par expected once it starts raising interest rates, which many\par investors expect will begin in the second quarter of 2015.\par     An increase in U.S. borrowing costs and rises in U.S. bond\par yields can dampen the appeal of higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par assets.\par     "I think market will buy U.S. dollar on any dips now. The\par only trend is buy U.S. dollar," said a trader for a Malaysian\par bank in Kuala Lumpur.\par     The trader added that the liquidation of long dollar\par positions helped some Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  edge higher on Tuesday.\par     In addition to the dollar's strength, the rupiah has been\par hit by concerns about political uncertainty.    \par     The rupiah has extended its losses after Indonesia's\par parliament on Friday approved a measure ending direct elections\par for governors and mayors, a move president-elect Joko Widodo\par criticised as a "big step back" for democracy in the country.\par  \par     "We think expectations that his large margin of victory in\par the popular vote would make it easy for President-elect Joko\par (Widodo) to govern need to be scaled back. The baseline scenario\par that is shaping up is political gridlock," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tim Condon\f28 \uc2 \f2 , head of\par research Asia for \f28 \uc2 \f2 ING\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a research note.\par     \par     SOUTH KOREAN WON\par     The won edged up 0.2 percent to 1052.00 versus the dollar\par , regaining some footing after slipping to 1057.2\par earlier on Tuesday, its lowest level since early April.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should\par upward pressure on interest rates become too great or the won\par exchange rate become too volatile after the Fed starts raising\par interest rates, a senior official said. \par     \par     SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par     The Singapore dollar edged up 0.2 percent to 1.2728 versus\par the dollar, having pulled up from a low of 1.2768 touched on\par Monday, the Singapore dollar's lowest level in six months. \par     Month-end position adjustment helped lend support to the\par Singapore dollar on Tuesday. \par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR     \par                                        \par   Change on the day at   0453 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       109.30         109.50       +0.18\par   Sing dlr        1.2728         1.2749       +0.16\par   Taiwan dlr      30.451         30.502       +0.17\par   Korean won     1052.00        1053.80       +0.17\par   Baht             32.35          32.38       +0.09\par   Peso             44.83          45.00       +0.37\par   Rupiah        12160.00       12165.00       +0.04\par   Rupee            61.65          61.51       -0.23\par   Ringgit         3.2660         3.2800       +0.43\par   Yuan            6.1457         6.1501       +0.07\par                                     \par   Change so far in 2014             \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       109.30         105.28       -3.68\par   Sing dlr        1.2728         1.2632       -0.75\par   Taiwan dlr      30.451         29.950       -1.65\par   Korean won     1052.00        1055.40       +0.32\par   Baht             32.35          32.86       +1.58\par   Peso             44.83          44.40       -0.97\par   Rupiah        12160.00       12160.00       +0.00\par   Rupee            61.65          61.80       +0.25\par   Ringgit         3.2660         3.2755       +0.29\par   Yuan            6.1457         6.0539       -1.49\par                                     \par  --------------------------------   \par               \par \par  (Additional reporting b