\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korean shares edge up with eye on \uc2 Fed\uc2  meeting; won firmer\b0\par\par\uc2 482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2014\par 04:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * KOSPI rises, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daewoo\f28 \uc2 \f2  Shipbuilding up 2 pct on LNG ship\par order\par     * Won rebounds from 5-week low, strong resistance at 1,040\par \par     By Joonhee Yu\par     SEOUL, Sept 16 (Reuters) - South Korean shares edged up in\par narrow trade on Tuesday morning, with investors cautious ahead\par of the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy meeting that will set a\par clearer direction for the market.\par     The Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) was up\par 0.32 percent at 2,042.37 points as of 0145 GMT, posting a modest\par rebound after coming within a whisker of a 2-month low in the\par previous session. \par     The Fed's Open Market Committee will begin its regular\par two-day policy meeting later on Tuesday, and investors will be\par scanning the outcome for clues on the timing of the first U.S.\par rate hike in more than eight years. \par     "Even though the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  isn't expected to take any significant\par action in the near term, market players are still wary of any\par comments that might provide stronger hints of an early rate\par hike," said Bae Sung-jin, an analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hyundai Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daewoo Shipbuilding & Marine Engineering\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares\par rose 2 percent after saying it had won a $160 million order to\par supply two LNG carriers for an unnamed buyer in\par Asia. \par     Shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Korea Electric Power Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 KEPCO\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) \par tumbled 2.9 percent after saying on Monday after trading hours\par that it had selected four brokerages to oversee the sale of some\par of its shares to restructure its finances.\par     The South Korean won was firmer, bouncing back from a\par 5-week intraday low on Monday, with exporters unloading the\par dollar for settlements and providing stiff resistance at the\par 1,040 level.\par     The won was quoted at 1,033.7 to the dollar as of\par 0145 GMT, compared with Monday's close of 1,038.0.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 South Korea's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief said on Tuesday that the\par won was facing a conflicting set of external factors including a\par possible rise in U.S. interest rates over the coming months and\par more easing steps from European and Japanese\par policymakers. \par     The local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has retreated nearly 2 percent this month\par after skirting a 6-year high against the dollar on Sept. 2,\par tracking the greenback's strength against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such\par as the yen and euro and further weighed by concerns of possible\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's financial authorities.\par     September futures on three-year treasury bonds rose\par 3 basis points to trade at 107.38. \par     \par                        0145 GMT    Prev close\par  Dollar/won             1,033.7       1,038.0\par  Yen/won             9.6673/755        9.6847\par  *KTB futures            107.38        107.35\par  KOSPI                 2,042.37      2,035.82\par  * Front-month futures on three-year treasury bonds\par \par  (Reporting by Joonhee Yu; Editing by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jacqueline Wong\f28 \uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-16T03:32:46.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140916ea9g001uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
