\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korean shares hit 3-week low on global demand worries, won firms\b0\par\par\uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2014\par 04:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * KOSPI lowest since Aug 13 as global demand worries, ECB\par uncertainty weigh\par     * Won stronger on exporter settlements\par \par     SEOUL, Sept 3 (Reuters) - South Korean shares slipped to a\par three-week low on Wednesday morning, hurt by nagging worries\par over underperforming exports and uncertainty on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy decision  later this week. \par     French President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Francois Hollande\f28 \uc2 \f2  and ECB chief Mario\par Draghi agreed on Monday that deflation and weak growth were\par threatening the euro zone economy - a key destination for South\par Korean exporters.\par     "It's hard to see the ECB implementing something as drastic\par as QE when its long-term refinancing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are just\par starting to kick in, and that's not even taking into account the\par political opposition, especially in Germany" said Ko Seung-hee,\par an analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 SK Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     The Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) was\par down 0.11 percent at 2,049.28 points as of 0155 GMT, but off an\par earlier low of 2,040.44 - the weakest level since Aug. 13.\par     The KOSPI has lost ground in the last three sessions due to\par a combination of geopolitical tensions, tepid exports and weak\par manufacturing activity in much of Asia and Europe.\par     Investors have sought out traditional defensive sectors,\par with telecoms and utilities outperforming a broadly weaker\par market.\par     Shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 SK Telecom\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the nation's largest\par mobile services provider, rose 2.5 percent, while third-largest\par provider \f28 \uc2 \f2 LG Uplus\f28 \uc2 \f2  gained 2.2 percent.\par     The South Korean won firmed as exporters sold dollars\par for settlements ahead of a long holiday weekend. Some traders\par were also scaling back their dollar exposure after the U.S.\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  posted its largest daily percentage gain in two weeks\par against the won on Tuesday.\par     The won was quoted at 1,017.4 to the dollar as of\par 0155 GMT, compared to Tuesday's closing rate of 1,018.3.\par     Traders have become wary of an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by monetary\par authorities after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  warned against the won's rapid\par rise, especially against the yen, as the firmer won put an extra\par burden on a trade-reliant economy already hit by sustained\par weakness in consumer spending. \par     September futures on three-year treasury bonds fell\par 6 basis points to trade at 106.95.\par     \par                        0155 GMT    Prev close\par  Dollar/won             1,017.4       1,018.3\par  Yen/won              9.6598/72        9.6898\par  *KTB futures            106.95        107.01\par  KOSPI                 2,049.28      2,051.58\par  * Front-month futures on three-year treasury bonds\par \par  (Reporting By Joonhee Yu; Editing by Shri Navaratnam)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-3T03:42:15.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140903ea93002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
