\par\par \b \uc2 South Korean Authorities Warn on Won Surge\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kwanwoo Jun \par \uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2014\par 12:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 SEOUL--South Korea's financial authorities on Wednesday issued a rare warning about the surging Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it hit a six-year high against the dollar, which threatens to further erode the nation's export competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won has risen about 10% in the past year and is the best performing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 2014. On Wednesday, it rose as high as 1,009.2 against the dollar in local trading, its highest level since July 2008.\par \par \uc2 The won's steep rise is due in part to a growing appetite among foreign investors--in search of security and solid returns--for South Korean debt and other assets. The country has had a sizable current-account surplus since March 2012 and its exports of mobile handsets, cars and memory chips continue to be brisk thanks to overseas demand.\par \par \uc2 In a joint message Wednesday, \uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2  and the finance ministry said they were concerned "about the possibility of overly one-sided moves among market participants" saying financial authorities were watching for any unusual trading patterns. The joint "verbal" warning--the first in eight months--put traders on watch for possible won-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Asia's fourth-largest economy, relies heavily on exports for economic growth and has often been chided for keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  intentionally low to make its goods cheaper in global markets. But analysts note that financial authorities haven't been suspected of ordering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months. The government has said it only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce market volatility.\par \par \uc2 Samsung Futures \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Jeon Seung-ji said Seoul's patience about the won's appreciation would be put to the test if the dollar-won exchange dropped to 1,000. A lower number means a stronger won.\par \par \uc2 "For now, financial authorities are still seen allowing the won to appreciate, but if the exchange rate approaches the important 1,000 level, they would take action aggressively," said Ms. Jeon.\par \par \uc2 The scheduled appointment of a new finance minister in July has raised expectations that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may become more common. Choi Kyung-hwan, the incoming finance minister, is widely seen as an advocate of policies to accelerate economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  economist Wai Ho Leong said he expects the government's sensitivity to the won's gain beyond 1,010 to increase "disproportionately" once Mr. Choi takes office.\par \par \uc2 "Once Mr. Choi is appointed later this month, he is likely to direct more decisive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remove one-sided appreciation expectations from the market," said Mr. Leong.\par \par \uc2 The won was relatively weak against a number of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  between the financial crisis and 2012, which allowed South Korea to increase its share of global trade.\par \par \uc2 Write to Kwanwoo Jun at kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 02, 2014 06:21 ET (10:21 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140702ea720013o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
