\par\par \b \uc2 USD/KRW Needs to Break 1,018-1,020 Technical Rut -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,565 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2014\par 03:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0144 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW needs to break out of the 1,018-1,020 technical rut in order to gain some momentum. On a Tuesday close below 1,018, the daily Bollinger downtrend channel comes into effect and could exert a downward force toward the channel's base at 1,015. But if the 1,020 round-figure trading barrier--also the 20-day Bollinger mid line resistance--is breached, USD/KRW could take on a mild bullish bias. The market remains wary of \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won rises too fast, which was hinted at by authorities recently. USD/KRW is now 1,018.0 from its Monday close of 1,018.5. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0137 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The supportive tone in USD/TWD continues as the pair again meets strong buying just below the 30.000 round-figure trading barrier - suspected to be from the \uc2 Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been propping up the U.S. dollar and deterring bullish bets on the Taiwan dollar. In the offshore nondeliverable forward market, the pricing of the benchmark 1-month contract suggests a neutral to mildly bullish sentiment for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On Monday, Taiwan's industrial output index hit a record high of 109.56 - a 5.19% yearly increase for May. The improvement reflects an increase in demand for consumer goods also seen in China's improved manufacturing PMI which was released the same day. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 UBS\uc2  reiterates its 5700 year-end target for Australia's S&P/ASX 200 based on its view of a sideways trend in the market multiple, mid-single-digit FY15 earnings growth, and an exchange rate fall to US$0.85 by year end. "We expect the A$ to resume its downward trend, based on solid US economic growth ultimately pushing up US bond yields and the US dollar," \uc2 UBS\uc2  strategists David Cassidy and Dean Dusanic say. "This should ultimately be positive for Australian corporate profits and the sustainability of the market uptrend, though it may well induce a global/local correction in the interim." The strategists add CBA (CBA.AU) to their model portfolio, expecting bad debts will be lower for longer, while also adding Breville (BVG.AU) after recent share price weakness. NAB (NAB.AU) is removed to make way for CBA, and Orora (ORA.AU) is removed on recent share price strength. Index last down 0.5% at 5426.9. (david.rogers@wsj.com; Twitter: @DavidRogersWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Nikkei is down 0.5% at 15292.02 Tuesday as profit-taking kicks in after the index gained 9.5% over the last month, and before Prime Minister Abe announces his economic revival plan later today. "Event-driven hedge funds may look at the Abe speech as a decent opportunity to short the market, as has been seen many times in the past when economic policy is announced by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 --especially as stocks have gained so much in such little time," says \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp\uc2 . strategist Daisuke Uno. "But no selloffs over the last month have been over 1.1%, so there is likely going to be a narrow window to pull the market down. On the contrary, if longer term investors keep buying into the market, the upside bias could remain for at least a few more months." (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 falls 0.5% to 5425.3 after Wall Street was unable to sustain gains despite stronger-than-expected U.S. existing home sales and manufacturing PMI data, along with a pullback in oil prices. Asian markets are in focus after failing to rally on above-consensus HSBC China manufacturing data Monday. "Asian markets fell a lot [Monday]...and closed on their lows," says an institutional trader at a major brokerage. Banks are underperforming, with \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU), ANZ (ANZ.AU) and NAB (NAB.AU) down 0.7% after U.S. bond yields rose slightly. Retail-sector profit warnings continue to flow, with Kathmandu (KMD.AU) down 11% after warning of a weather-related fall in profit. Of concern for mining services, The West Australian reports an external review of \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2 's (BHP.AU) iron-ore division has recommended the world's largest mining company lay off more than 3,000 workers. (david.rogers@wsj.com; Twitter: @DavidRogersWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Hong Kong equities are expected to recover from selling which followed yesterday's better-than-expected Chinese manufacturing data. \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's preliminary China manufacturing PMI for June released yesterday rose to a seven-month high of 50.8. After an early rally, the Hang Seng Index closed down 1.7% yesterday at 22,804.81, the biggest intraday decline for the index in three months. The PMI data suggest "the various policy easing measures over recent months are working rather well and having a greater-than-expected effect on growth," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . Technical indicators show some evidence of profit-taking in yesterday's selling, says RHB Retail Research, adding that "the trend is not turning bearish yet". Index futures are up 0.4% ahead of the market open. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; Twitter: @gregorhunter)\par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Zinc edges higher, outperforming other base metals in early Asia. Zinc prices remain at their highest level since February 2013. "Zinc prices have begun a broad rally, with markets re-shifting their focus back to this metal after the pullback in nickel prices recently," Jessica Fung, an analyst with BMO Capital Markets, says in a report. BMO expects a balanced-to-surplus market in zinc through 2016, but significant deficits thereafter. It expects zinc prices to average $0.94/pound ($2,072.34/ton) in 2014. LME 3-month zinc is at $2,185/ton, up $1 from its previous close, while all other base metals are in negative territory.(arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com; Twitter: @ArpanMukherjee)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The post-FOMC Fed speak will start this week, with Philadelphia Fed President Charles Plosser presenting his views on the economic outlook and monetary policy. A voting member this year, Plosser has been one of the most hawkish members of the FOMC, and \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  expects him to extend that rhetoric. Earlier this year, Plosser said he expected short-term U.S. rates to hit 3% at end-2015. Plosser is likely expecting rates to be above 4% by end-2016, it says. Plosser will highlight the Fed's progress on both sides of its mandate, employment and inflation, and reiterate his hawkish views on rates. Plosser speaks at the Economic Club of New York at 0805 ET Tuesday. (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Hyundai Motor Co\uc2 .'s (005380.SE) second-quarter earnings likely will meet market expectations when it unveils figures next month, says Meritz Securities analyst Kim Joon-seong. He expects \uc2 Hyundai\uc2 's 2Q sales to rise 1.3% on year to 23.5 trillion won ($23.1 billion) but operating profit to fall 7.3% to 2.23 trillion won, hurt by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation. The analyst rates the stock as buy and maintains a six-month target price of KRW330,000. \uc2 Hyundai\uc2  is up 0.5% at KRW220,500. (in-soo.nam@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0011 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  commodities research group revises higher its forecast for the Australian dollar to reflect the "recent sustained strength" of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is now around 8% higher in 2014 against the U.S. dollar. CS now expects the Australian dollar to average US$0.9200 in calendar 2014, up from an earlier forecast of US$0.8900. It also raises its forecasts for 2015 to US$0.8800 from US$0.8600. (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0002 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Offshore accounts at five foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 2.3 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders. The first day of premarket selling after four days of net buying may be negative for the market at the open, although the yen-value basis of the figures is unknown. Sell orders total 8.6 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 6.3 million shares. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2313 GMT (\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ) The Australian dollar has held its strong gains posted Monday on the back of the greather-than-50-point print on Chinese performance of manufacturing index, says Greg Moore, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Dominion Securities\uc2 . Mr. Moore adds that, technically speaking, the overnight close made a bullish outside-range day that suggests more gains are likely in store. The Australian dollar has risen by around 8% against the U.S. dollar so far in 2014, and in early Asia trading it remains just shy of its year-to-date high of US$0.9463, trading at US$0.9425. A break-up would see the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise to seventh-month high. (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 23, 2014 21:44 ET (01:44 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140624ea6o0007f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
