\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW-Bank of Korea to stand pat on policy Thursday, unworried by won at 6-year high\b0\par\par\uc2 By Choonsik Yoo   \par \uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2014\par 10:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, June 11 (Reuters) - \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to keep interest rates on hold on Thursday, unworried by the won's rise to six-year highs as it nears the symbolically important 1,000-per-dollar mark.\par \par \uc2 Whereas central banks often come under pressure to cut rates when a strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatens economic performance, comments by top South Korean officials and what traders say has been limited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets indicate a level of comfort among policymakers with its recent appreciation.\par \par \uc2 "What I've been noticing recently is that the government is not attempting to turn the won's direction but is only trying to slow the pace of increase," said Young Sun Kwon, economist at \uc2 Nomura International\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think the won will be a key factor (to \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2 ) going forward as the won's rise has been led by improving fundamentals in external balance positions," he said.\par \par \uc2 All 30 analysts surveyed by Reuters forecast \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  would hold its base rate steady at 2.50 percent for a 13th consecutive month on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-five of 27 analysts who offered a clear view on the next rate change predicted a 25-basis-point increase in the 7-day repurchase agreement rate within the next three months at the earliest as economic growth fuels price pressures.\par \par \uc2 Annual inflation in May picked up to a 19-month high of 1.7 percent. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a target of keeping annual inflation at between 2.5 percent and 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 The won has gained 11.6 percent against the dollar in the past year and ended domestic trade on Wednesday at 1,015.7 per dollar, close to breaching 1,000 for the first time in six years.\par \par \uc2 "The authorities accommodated market pressure for the won to appreciate in late March and early April. I expect they will behave similarly in the future," said \uc2 Tim Condon\uc2 , chief Asia economist at \uc2 ING\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 HUGE CURRENT ACCOUNT SURPLUS\par \par \uc2 The won's current appreciation is being driven by large and consistent current account surpluses combined with improving economic growth prospects, rather than a rush of short-term portfolio investment into local financial markets, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the portfolio investment account swung to a net outflow of $8.3 billion from an inflow of $6.7 billion in 2012, whereas the current account surplus shot up to 6.1 percent of annual gross domestic product from 4.2 percent in 2012.\par \par \uc2 Officials at the presidential office, finance ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , declining to be identified given the sensitivity of the matter, have told Reuters that the government was not attempting to reverse a rising won that is driven by strong economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor made it clear last month that the days have gone when authorities acted to keep the won cheap to help exporters at the expense of domestic sectors.\par \par \uc2 "(A stronger won) may have negative effects on the economy, but when you take domestic consumption into consideration, a stronger won has boosted spending power and it may bring about positive effects like raising weak domestic spending," Governor Lee Ju-yeol told a news conference on May 9.\par \par \uc2 It was unusual for a South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor to speak of the positive effects of a stronger won on the economy.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's economy, the fourth-largest in Asia, has been increasingly dependent for growth on exports of smartphones, cars and ships, but President Park Geun-hye has promised to reduce the dependence by strengthening domestic sectors.\par \par \uc2 Growing conflicts with the U.S. administration and the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  also leave South Korea with limited room in responding to the won's climb.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Department of Treasury and the IMF separately said early this year \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market mainly to resist the won's appreciation, adding the won as a result remained undervalued.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not provide any official data on market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but has said its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were always aimed at smoothing out the market's volatility. Traders have said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks was modest and limited. (Editing by Tony Munroe & Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-6-14T08:42:28.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140611ea6b004bl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
