\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2  Won Rises Vs USD, Mild  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Suspected -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,550 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2014\par 08:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0609 GMT [Dow Jones] The South Korea won strengthens to close at KRW1,020.5 versus the U.S. dollar late Thursday as exporters - who regularly repatriate overseas earnings - sold the U.S. dollar aggressively before the long weekend. South Korea markets will be closed Friday. It appears \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  - which has over the last three weeks been suspected of buying U.S. dollars intermittently in the spot market to prevent the won from rising excessively - may have stepped in again Thursday at around KRW1,020, according to a couple of traders. A near-term bearish technical bias has emerged for USD/KRW now that it has closed below 1,021.5 and therefore within the Bollinger downtrend channel. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0605 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares carve out a 0.2% gain to end at 9140.72 thanks to buying in tech names but analysts say the index is likely to stay rangebound in the near future due to lack of catalysts. "Players are watching for the outcome of the ECB meeting and U.S. non-farm payroll but no one is anticipating any surprises," says Alan Tseng at Mega Securities. Among tech stocks, Acer (2353.TW) rises to the 7% daily limit to NT$20.95 driven by its new cloud computing services, Mediatek (2454.TW) is up 3.36% to NT$507 while TSMC (2330.TW) falls 0.4% to NT$123. Taiex is tipped to test 9150 resistance Friday. (jenny.hsu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0551 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold is in a tight range as investors wait for the outcome of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting, due later in the global trading day. Some analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce stimulus measures that could strengthen the USD, making gold more expensive to holders of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Also, the U.S. non-farm payrolls data, due Friday, could provide further cues to gold investors. Based on technical indicators, gold prices could bounce in the short-term towards $1,280/oz region, which "might only be a blip in an otherwise downward trajectory," brokerage IG says in a report. Spot gold is at $1,243.96/oz, up 36 cents from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @ArpanMukherjee)\par \par \uc2 0526 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/AUD is a tad higher after the Australian trade data came in below expectations and weighed on the AUD, says \uc2 HiFX\uc2  Head of Trading Alex Hill. The pair is at 0.9092 vs. 0.9078 early this morning. Australia posted its first monthly trade deficit since November in April of A$122 million vs. expectations of a surplus of A$300 million. Mr. Hill adds, however, says the next major driver will be the RBNZ cash rate decision next week; "The market is starting to think it got ahead of itself (on the steepness of the increases) and we are certainly waiting for (the RBNZ Governor) to confirm that and you might see some more follow through when the do." Hill puts support for the pair at 0.9050 with resistance coming in at 0.9180. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;\uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 0523 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR rises to 59.33 from Wednesday's close of 59.27, reinforcing the uptrend line projected from the one-year low recorded on May 22. This technical support at 59.08, which coincides with the 20-day Bollinger mid support line, provides a springboard off which USD/INR could rally even higher - possibly to the round-figure trading barrier at 60.00. A Thursday close above 59.53 would activate the daily Bollinger uptrend channel and add more bullish technical bias to USD/INR. The underlying driver for U.S. dollar strength has been the rising level of the benchmark U.S. 10-year Treasury yield - currently just above 2.60%. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0513 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 closes flat at 5159.49 as there are few cues in the local market and volumes remain light, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker, Alexandra Dalzell. She says that a softer NZD/USD and NZD/AUD was helping exporters such as Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ), who ended up 2.2% at NZ$4.60. She says retirement village operator, Ryman (RYM.NZ) continues to rebound after losing ground following its FY14 results as investors looking for value move back into the stock. Ryman closes up 1.3% at NZ$8.35. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;\uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 0508 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  won't be changing its tight policy stance, Gov. Agus Martowardojo says. "In general BI will keep its monetary position," he says meeting expectations of economists who say it will leave its benchmark rate unchanged at 7.5% when it meets next week. This is because of the affects on the rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from seasonal dollar demand and the July presidential elections. (Farida.husna@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold Y646.8 billion of 1.7% 30-year bonds, and the result showed the lowest price at 99.65, yielding 1.717%. The outcome was mixed. While the lowest price matched market expectations, other measures of demand got worse, with the bid-to-cover ratio down to 3.00 from 4.62 in the previous tender on May 13, and the tail longer at 0.07 vs. 0.03. Still, "the result was relatively solid," as there had been speculation that demand would weaken more on worries that the BOJ might reduce the weight of superlong debt in its bond-purchase program, says Teruyoshi Sotome, a bond strategist at Mizuho Securities. He also notes that the amount sold this time is about Y100 billion larger than in May. Lead June JGB futures are flat at 145.33. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com )\par \par \uc2 0445 GMT [Dow Jones] Hong Kong stocks are mixed after Thursday's HSBC China services Purchasing Manager's Index for May shows the slowest pace of expansion in four months, although mainland Chinese stocks advance as stimulus expectations rise. The Hang Seng Index is down 0.2% to 23,101.95, while H-shares tracked by the Hang Seng China Enterprises Index gain 0.1% to 10,329.06. A gauge of Hong Kong-listed real estate developers rises 0.8% as investment banks including \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  and Bank of Communications turn positive on the sector. "Developers have outperformed on positive newsflow in the physical market and we expect good sales will continue to serve as a share price catalyst," says UOBKayHian, increasing its target price on \uc2 Cheung Kong Holdings\uc2  (0001.HK) by 8.2% and New World Development (0017.HK) by 25.6%. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @gregorhunter)\par \par \uc2 0430 GMT [Dow Jones] Falling spot prices for iron ore and coal--which combined make up 30% of Australia's exports--will pressure Australian export income in coming months, says Kieran Davies, chief economist at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , based in Sydney. Davies said the iron ore price has dropped 12% in Australian dollar terms since December, while coal has fallen 18%. He adds that he expects commodity volumes will continue to grow at a rapid rate as more major mining projects finish, which is likely to place further downward pressure on commodity prices. Declining prices should more than offset higher volumes. (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0430 GMT [Dow Jones] Earnings per share growth of India's benchmark Sensex index is likely to reach 16% in both FY15 and FY16 on the back of political stability and an improved earnings outlook, Antique Stock Broking Ltd. says. That compares with 8% growth in FY14, it adds. "We believe that formation of BJP-led government at the centre will meaningfully improve 'business confidence' and boost investment cycle, leading to higher GDP growth in FY16 onwards," the brokerage says. It recommends buying banks, industrials, automobiles, cement, and energy sector shares as these sectors still trade at a meaningful discount to their 'highs' in terms of valuations. The Sensex is currently trading down 0.5% at 24681.38 points. The brokerage is maintaining an index target of 27712 by March 2015. (debi.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0422 GMT [Dow Jones] The Intercontinental Exchange Dollar Index is hovering Thursday not far below a near-four-month high of 80.68 hit on Monday as the markets await the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest rate decision due at 1145 GMT. Dow Jones technical analysis shows the Dollar Index on course to reach the 81.324-81.388 band, marked by the Jan. 31 and Jan. 21 reaction highs, as the five- and 15-day moving averages are advancing. Minor resistance could be encountered at the Feb. 12 high of 80.831. However, the anticipated Dollar Index advance would be deferred if it moves below May 30 minor reaction low of 80.312. The spot Dollar Index was recently at 80.64 compared with 80.66 in late New York Wednesday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 05, 2014 02:09 ET (06:09 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140605ea65000ok\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
