\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Falling yuan weighs on Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , but most set to see good April\b0\par\par\uc2 884 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2014\par 07:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Ringgit down on dollar short-covering before Fed\par     * S.Korea \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected, won tad lower\par     * Won to see best month in seven months\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, April 30 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  eased on Wednesday as the Chinese yuan lost ground\par and on a broad dollar strength ahead of the result of a U.S.\par Federal Reserve policy meeting, but many regional units were set\par to see monthly gains on capital inflows.\par     Investors hesitated to build up aggressive positions before\par key data from major economies such as U.S. jobs numbers on\par Friday and China's manufacturing activity index on Thursday.\par Most Asian financial markets will be closed on Thursday for the\par Labou Day holiday.\par     The yuan hit a 18-month low against the dollar\par after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixed its daily mid-point weaker and on\par increasing corporate dollar demand.\par     Offshore spot yuan also fell to its lowest since\par October 2012.\par     Taiwan's financial regulator is seeking to crack down on\par banks' sales of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  derivatives amid increasing client\par complaints about losses on yuan investment products after the\par renminbi's recent slide.  \par     The dollar rose against a basket of six major\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with the euro down after slower-than-expected German\par inflation.\par     The Malaysian ringgit led daily losses among\par emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as investors cut bullish positions.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's won eased, with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par authorities suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem its appreciation\par and on dollar demand from local importers.\par     Still, most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were poised to record\par monthly gains and they may appreciate further in May even though\par investors are awaiting the outcome of the Fed's two-day policy\par meeting later in the day.\par     "All bodes well with searching for yields," said Andy Ji,\par Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  in\par Singapore, adding the Indian rupee and the Indonesian\par rupiah may lead regional gains once uncertainties about\par elections in those countries ease.\par     "So even with sell in May in terms of risk sentiment, I\par don't see USD/Asia get a significant lift. Broad USD weakness is\par going to linger," Ji said, referring to U.S. dollar/Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs.\par     In April, the won outperformed emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par having risen 3.1 percent against the dollar. That would mark its\par largest monthly gain since September last year.\par     Stocks and bond inflows attracted by strong economic\par fundamentals have supported the South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The Philippine peso has gained 0.6 percent while\par the Thai baht has appreciated 0.5 percent.\par     Singapore's dollar and the Taiwan dollar \par have each risen 0.1 percent. \par     However, the yuan has lost 0.8 percent in April, set to\par suffer a fourth consecutive month of depreciation which have\par more than erased last year's gain.\par     The rupiah has fallen 1.7 percent and the rupee has dipped\par 0.7 percent as investors took profits from the two best\par performing emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far this year on\par election uncertainties.\par         \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit fell as most of its non-deliverable forwards\par  eased on selling from U.S. names, traders said.\par     Local importers also bought the dollar for payments.\par     The Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also came under pressure from daily\par fixing-related dollar demand and as some investors sold the\par ringgit against the Singapore dollar.\par     \par     TAIWAN DOLLAR\par     The Taiwan dollar gained as exporters bought it for\par month-end settlements, while trading was subdued as local\par financial markets are closed on Thursday for the Labour Day.\par     The island's economy grew in the first quarter at its\par fastest pace in over a year as strong domestic consumption\par helped offset exports, preliminary data showed earlier.\par  \par     Some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders, however, said the data did not help\par the Taiwan dollar much as investors are still watching if the\par growth is sustainable.\par     The Taiwan dollar's upside was limited by a weaker yuan and\par as investors were also awaiting the result of the Fed policy\par meeting later in the day.\par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0450 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       102.41         102.64       +0.22\par   Sing dlr        1.2563         1.2560       -0.02\par   Taiwan dlr      30.209         30.250       +0.14\par   Korean won     1032.20        1030.60       -0.16\par   Baht             32.26          32.28       +0.05\par   Peso             44.53          44.50       -0.07\par   Rupiah        11555.00       11545.00       -0.09\par   Rupee            60.36          60.42       +0.10\par   Ringgit         3.2650         3.2585       -0.20\par   Yuan            6.2660         6.2580       -0.13\par  \par   Change so far in 2014\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       102.41         105.28       +2.80\par   Sing dlr        1.2563         1.2632       +0.55\par   Taiwan dlr      30.209         29.950       -0.86\par   Korean won     1032.20        1055.40       +2.25\par   Baht             32.26          32.86       +1.86\par   Peso             44.53          44.40       -0.30\par   Rupiah        11555.00       12160.00       +5.24\par   Rupee            60.36          61.80       +2.39\par   Ringgit         3.2650         3.2755       +0.32\par   Yuan            6.2660         6.0539       -3.38\par     \par \par  (Additionbal reporting by Miao-jung Lin in TAIPEI; Editing by\par Kim Coghill)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-5-3T05:28:39.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140430ea4u009st\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
