\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-S.Korea extends current account surplus streak in March\b0\par\par\uc2 405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2014\par 02:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds new details, official comments)\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, April 29 (Reuters) - South Korea posted its 34th consecutive current account surplus in March, favoured by resilient exports and the low prices of imported raw materials, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Asia's fourth-largest economy posted a hefty current account surplus of $6.65 billion in March on a seasonally adjusted basis, down from a revised $7.54 billion surplus in February, but still substantial.\par \par \uc2 "The surplus trend will be maintained...and the current account is still on the path we forecast earlier in April," said Jung Joon, director of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary and financial statistics division.\par \par \uc2 South Korea is now on course to post a current account surplus of nearly $70 billion for the year after recording a surplus of $79.9 billion last year, which amounted to more than 6 percent of annual gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 The continuing current account surplus adds to upward pressure on the won, which has been trading near six-year highs against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's terms of trade have improved as global commodity prices have fallen and South Korea's export competitiveness has strengthened, jacking up the value of the won.\par \par \uc2 That rise has worried policymakers and has frequently led to market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the form of "smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  and the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  have both voiced concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in recent economic reports, but local authorities have not offered a formal reply.\par \par \uc2 Without adjusting for seasonal variance, South Korea posted a current account surplus of $7.35 billion in March, up from a $4.50 billion surplus in February, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed.\par \par \uc2 Exports fell 0.2 percent to $52.36 billion in March from February on a seasonally adjusted basis, and imports rose 1.4 percent to $45.13 billion, \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  data showed, resulting in a goods account surplus of $7.23 billion.\par \par \uc2 In the financial account, South Korea saw a net outflow of $5.78 billion in March, compared to a revised net outflow of $6.92 billion in February, the data showed. Financial-account figures are not adjusted for seasonal patterns. (Reporting by Christine Kim; Editing by Choonsik Yoo and Eric Meijer) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-4-29T01:53:27.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140429ea4t00105\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
