\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-IMF adds pressure on South Korea to let won rise\b0\par\par\uc2 460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2014\par 05:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts throughout with more details)\par \par \uc2 By Choonsik Yoo\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, April 18 (Reuters) - South Korea came under fresh pressure on Friday to let the won rise faster, when the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said it was carrying an abnormally big current account surplus and that more dollar purchases would do more harm than good.\par \par \uc2 The IMF said in a report based on its late 2013 annual policy review that the won was estimated to be as much as 8 percent undervalued, while saying \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively to resist the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  also called on South Korea in a scheduled report early this week to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to exceptional circumstances and not use regulatory measures to curb appreciation pressures.\par \par \uc2 "Staff, therefore, continue to assess that the exchange rate remains moderately undervalued in the range of 2-8 percent, with the upper end of the range seeming more plausible in light of the recent widening of the current account surplus," the IMF said said.\par \par \uc2 The won has gained 1.8 percent against the dollar so far this year and was trading close to a near six-year high set last week.\par \par \uc2 The IMF said South Korea's current account surplus was estimated to be around 3-4 percent more than the norm. South Korea's current account surplus shot up to 6.1 percent of annual gross domestic product in 2013 from 4.2 percent in 2012.\par \par \uc2 "Over the medium term, greater exchange rate appreciation would encourage reallocation of resources to the non-tradables sector, thereby further supporting rebalancing," the IMF said, adding further dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also costly.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy, the fourth-largest in Asia, relies heavily on exports for growth and the country has repeatedly denied it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with the aim of keeping the won cheaper than the fair value.\par \par \uc2 "(South Korean authorities) disagreed with staff's assessment that the won is moderately undervalued, noting its appreciation since 2012," the IMF also said in the report. "They stressed that smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are restricted to alleviating excessive exchange rate volatility in the face of market herding."\par \par \uc2 The report was published based on the IMF's annual policy consultations with South Korea held in late 2013 and on a staff report prepared early this year, it said.\par \par \uc2 It added inflation in South Korea was expected to return to the country's target, which is set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at between 2.5 percent and 3.5 percent. Inflation has stayed below the lower end of the target for months. (Editing by \uc2 Jacqueline Wong\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-4-21T03:42:51.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140418ea4i003h2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
