\par\par \b \uc2 Slackening Growth Pressures Beijing -5-\b0\par\par\uc2 1,423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2014\par 07:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By BOB DAVIS , WILLIAM MAULDIN and MARK MAGNIER\par \par \uc2 BEIJING--China's economic growth slowed to its weakest pace in 18 months, putting pressure on China's leaders to allow the yuan to fall as a way to help troubled exporters and boost growth, and setting up a potential fight with the U.S. on trade and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Beijing released its disappointing growth figures hours after the U.S. blasted China for its recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, calling the decline in the yuan "unprecedented" and pressing Beijing to disclose its market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  more regularly.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department, in its semiannual report on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  released Tuesday, said the weakening of the yuan would "raise particularly serious concerns" if it signals a retreat from Beijing's publicly stated policy of scaling back \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to let market forces play a bigger role.\par \par \uc2 The report stopped short of labeling China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator, a move U.S. administrations have avoided for the past two decades. But the warning marked the Obama administration's latest attempt to keep Chinese officials from slipping on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commitments despite slower growth in the world's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 China's 7.4% year-on-year growth for the year's first quarter was below the 7.7% level seen in the fourth quarter of 2013, and slightly below the target of "about 7.5%" set by China's leadership for all of 2014.\par \par \uc2 In only one other quarter since the global financial crisis of 2009--the three months ending Sept. 30, 2012--did China miss the annual target, and that slowdown prompted Beijing to ease credit significantly in an effort to boost growth.\par \par \uc2 The rate, however, was slightly above economists' expectations, so Beijing may feel it has less need to prevent the yuan from rising. Many analysts expect China to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to begin appreciating again later in the year.\par \par \uc2 Since Feb. 17, when the yuan started heading downward, the yuan has weakened 2.5% against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 A main point of contention over the yuan's level against the dollar is whether China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which in recent years has strengthened, has risen to its natural strength or has further room to rise, as the U.S. contends.\par \par \uc2 American manufacturers have complained that China's artificially weak yuan gives the country an advantage in trade. And U.S. lawmakers have backed legislation targeting overseas \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation and asked the Obama administration in recent months to include \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules in trade negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Beijing had allowed the yuan to appreciate for years before shifting toward a recent bout of depreciation. This year's shift has been "quite unusual," said \uc2 Nicholas Lardy\uc2 , a China expert at the Peterson Institute for International Economics.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. administration had previously touted its efforts to persuade China to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate and want to make sure China isn't ignoring the recent weakness. Chinese officials and have said the yuan's weakness is a temporary move designed to halt hot-money bets on the strengthening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Reduced momentum in investment and consumption--key drivers of the economy--were behind the moderately weaker quarterly growth.\par \par \uc2 China also released a slew of other economic statistics on Wednesday that suggested continuing weakness. Industrial production grew 8.8% in March, below analyst expectations of 9.0%. This compares with 8.6% year-on-year growth in January and February, combined to limit distortions from the Lunar New Year holiday, according to the \uc2 National Bureau of Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The purchasing managers' index, another indicator of the health of the manufacturing sector released earlier this month, also showed some weakness.\par \par \uc2 Fixed asset investment, covering areas such as machinery, land and buildings, grew 17.6% in the first quarter, compared with 17.9% year on year in January-February, which analysts attributed in part to problems in the housing sector.\par \par \uc2 Retail sales, meanwhile, posted 12.2% year-on-year growth for March in line with the consensus, a modest increase over the 11.8% year-on-year rise seen in January and February. An anti-extravagance campaign introduced last year to stem corruption continues to impede spending, although tepid government support for e-commerce could goose consumers into opening their wallets.\par \par \uc2 A strengthening yuan cuts in different ways for Chinese growth. On the one hand a weaker yuan makes Chinese exports cheaper in dollar terms and gives Chinese manufacturers an edge. On the other hand, a stronger yuan makes imports cheaper in yuan terms and can boost consumer spending somewhat. It also helps China make progress in its goal of remaking its economy so it depends more on domestic consumption and less on exports.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, Chinese officials have tended to put concerns about short-term growth ahead of longer-term restructuring.\par \par \uc2 China tightly controls the value of the yuan and uses its massive foreign reserves to buy or sell yuan as a way to guide its direction. In the first quarter, those reserves jumped by $129 billion, hitting an all-time high of $3.95 trillion. The increase, analysts say, reflected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive down the yuan's value against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 David Loevinger, a former \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  official who is now an analyst at investment firm \uc2 TCW Group Inc\uc2 ., said U.S. complaints may have limited impact in China, which is focusing first on growth. Senior Chinese officials believe that unless they can keep GDP growth above a certain level--somewhere between 7% and 7.5%--unemployment may rise to unacceptable levels. "Complaints from the U.S. are going to increasingly fall on deaf ears, given the appreciation [of the yuan] in past few years and the adjustment in China's current account," a broad measure of a country's trade and financial flows.\par \par \uc2 One additional bit of leverage the U.S. may have this year is that China hosts the \uc2 Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation\uc2  Forum this November. Beijing tends to take such assignments seriously and tries to clear away disputes that could mar the event--and that could include a fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and trade policy. Indeed, a number of foreign and Chinese officials are lobbying Beijing to put free trade at the center of the APEC agenda.\par \par \uc2 The latest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report underscored the continued friction among the world's leading economies almost five years into a weak global recovery. Emerging economies have complained that the Federal Reserve's bond-buying program, known as quantitative easing, initially put downward pressure on the dollar and sent a flood of capital their way.\par \par \uc2 As the U.S. sees economic growth accelerating slowly, the Fed is paring back its easy-money policies--and generating more complaints overseas.\par \par \uc2 "The tough words in the report signal an escalation in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tensions among the major advanced and emerging market economies," said Cornell University economist \uc2 Eswar Prasad\uc2 , a former head of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's China division. "The U.S. has started pulling back on quantitative easing but other countries including China and Japan are continuing to rely on monetary policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation to buffer short-term growth."\par \par \uc2 The report said disclosing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market more regularly would help "to increase the credibility of its monetary policy framework and to promote exchange-rate and financial-market transparency."\par \par \uc2 The report maintained its criticism of some European policies as the euro zone faces slow growth and a persistent risk of deflation, or falling prices. Six months ago, the Treasury report employed unusually sharp language in criticizing Germany's economic policies and blamed the "anemic pace of domestic demand growth" for dragging down its neighbors and the rest of the global economy. This month's report registered the same concerns but in less strident language.\par \par \uc2 As with Europe, the U.S. said it was "imperative" for Japan to focus on an increase in domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 Finance Ministry officials in Japan said they concurred with the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 's assessment of the issues facing Japanese policy makers.\par \par \uc2 "Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 's economic policy is exactly aimed at fostering domestic demand-led growth," said a senior government official.\par \par \uc2 The report said \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  should limit won \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to exceptional circumstances. \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Embassy didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 --Eric Morath, Liyan Qi, Lingling Wei and Mitsuru Obe contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Bob Davis at bob.davis@wsj.com and William Mauldin at william.mauldin@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 16-04-14 0555GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140416ea4g0005x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
