\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Blasts China Over Yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 By William Mauldin   \par \uc2 378 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2014\par 00:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Obama administration blasted China for its recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, saying the decline in the yuan was "unprecedented" and calling on Beijing to disclose its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets more regularly.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2 , in its semiannual report on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  released late Tuesday, amplified its recent warnings by saying the weakening of the yuan would "raise particularly serious concerns" if it signals a retreat from Beijing's public policy of scaling back \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to let market forces play a bigger role.\par \par \uc2 "The Chinese authorities have been unwilling to allow an appreciation large enough to bring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to market equilibrium, opting instead for a gradual adjustment which has now been partially reversed," the Treasury Departing said in the report. "China should disclose \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to increase the credibility of its monetary policy framework and to promote exchange-rate and financial-market transparency."\par \par \uc2 The report stopped short of labeling China or any other country a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator. It also took a softer stance toward some policies in Europe. Six months ago, the Treasury employed unusually sharp language in openly criticizing Germany's economic policies and blamed the eurozone powerhouse for dragging down its neighbors and the rest of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department said Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  should limit won \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to exceptional circumstances. It also said Japan's economic policies should focus on boosting domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has said yuan is approaching its fair-market value, pointing to the country's shrinking trade surpluses. Last month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Vice Governor \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2  told an economic forum in Beijing that in the future the yuan's exchange rate "will be more and more determined by the market" and the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 's "decisive role in the exchange rate will weaken."\par \par \uc2 China hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  much in the market lately, while the direction of the yuan has headed downward due to market pressures. A broad range of economic indicators--from trade to consumer spending to industrial production--have showed a slowing Chinese economy, which has reduced demand for the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Write to William Mauldin at william.mauldin@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 15-04-14 2258GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140415ea4f000dz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
