\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won leads Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  weekly gains, but rupiah down\b0\par\par\uc2 497 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2014\par 10:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Updates prices. For midday report, double-click\par  )\par     SINGAPORE, April 11 (Reuters) - The South Korean won led\par weekly gains among emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with funds flowing\par into the region's battered stocks and bonds, but the Indonesian\par rupiah on Friday hit a one-month low on election disappointment.\par     The won ended local trading up 0.5 percent at 1,035.0 per\par dollar, its strongest domestic market close since August 2008,\par on exporters' demand for settlements.\par     Foreign investors extended their buying spree in Seoul's\par main stock market to a 13th consecutive session, providing more\par support to the won.\par     Traders said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities were\par spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow the appreciation of the won, the\par best-performing emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the week.\par     For the week, the won rose 1.8 percent against the dollar on\par continuous demand from offshore funds and exporters. That was\par the largest monthly appreciation since December 2011.\par     The Philippine peso gained 1.5 percent on inflows\par amid strong February exports and January foreign direct\par investments data.\par     Malaysia's ringgit has advanced 1.5 percent as\par funds and interbank speculators chased it with lower \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S.\par Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields.\par     The Singapore dollar has appreciated 0.9 percent as\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is widely expected to maintain its tight\par monetary policy at its semiannual policy meeting on Monday.\par     The Indonesian rupiah, however, has lost 0.8\par percent on political uncertainties after an unconvincing victory\par by the main opposition party in parliamentary elections on\par Wednesday.\par     Earlier on Friday, the rupiah fell 1.0 percent to 11,465 per\par dollar, its weakest since March 12, but pared much of the loss\par as Jakarta shares rebounded.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0800 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       101.73         101.53       -0.20\par   Sing dlr        1.2472         1.2500       +0.22\par   Taiwan dlr      30.122         30.130       +0.03\par   Korean won     1035.15        1040.20       +0.49\par   Baht             32.28          32.20       -0.25\par   Peso             44.28          44.31       +0.07\par   Rupiah        11410.00       11350.00       -0.53\par   Rupee            60.23          60.07       -0.27\par   Ringgit         3.2315         3.2230       -0.26\par   Yuan            6.2112         6.2125       +0.02\par  \par   Change so far in 2014\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       101.73         105.28       +3.48\par   Sing dlr        1.2472         1.2632       +1.28\par   Taiwan dlr      30.122         29.950       -0.57\par   Korean won     1035.15        1055.40       +1.96\par   Baht             32.28          32.86       +1.80\par   Peso             44.28          44.40       +0.26\par   Rupiah        11410.00       12160.00       +6.57\par   Rupee            60.23          61.80       +2.61\par   Ringgit   