\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories of the Day\b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2014\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA MANUFACTURING SLIPS, GAUGE SHOWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economy started the year on a weaker note, as an initial gauge of manufacturing activity in January slipped to its lowest level in six months. (published 0339 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SOUTH KOREA GROWTH SLOWS SLIGHTLY IN LAST QUARTER OF 2013 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's economic growth moderated in the final quarter of 2013, but a strong rebound in exports and improving domestic demand point to a recovery in Asia's fourth-largest economy this year. (published 0534 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SINGAPORE DECEMBER CPI RISES LESS THAN EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's consumer prices rose at a slower-than-expected pace in December, due to a decline in private road-transport costs. (published 0500 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par IMF QUESTIONS SOUTH KOREA'S \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Wednesday questioned \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, telling Seoul that it should only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in exchange-rate markets to prevent volatility that might damage the economy. (published 2317 GMT Wednesday) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UK 2013 CAR PRODUCTION HITS HIGHEST LEVEL SINCE 2007 - SMMT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. car production rose 3.1% last year to its highest level since 2007, driven by home demand and exports outside Europe, putting the resurgent British industry on course to overtake France as the continent's third-biggest car-making country. (published 0001 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NZ MANUFACTURING ACTIVITY EXPANDS FOR 15TH MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's manufacturing activity expanded for a fifteenth consecutive month in December, although activity slowed slightly from the previous month, data released Thursday showed. (published 2201 GMT Wednesday) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ARGENTINE LEADER RE-EMERGES, BUT FAILS TO CALM NERVES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Cristina Kirchner made her first public comments in nearly six weeks on Wednesday, but avoided mention of the growing economic problems that have put many Argentines on edge and led her approval ratings to tank. (published 0059 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM TO FINALISE FREE TRADE AGREEMENT WITH SOUTH KOREA THIS YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam and South Korea are working to finalise a free trade agreement later this year, which will aim to increase annual bilateral trade to $70 billion by 2020 from $27.3 billion last year. (published 0354 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par U.S. SEES ASIA-PACIFIC TRADE TALKS RESUMING NEXT MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 High-level negotiations to form an Asia-Pacific trade agreement likely will resume next month, a top U.S. trade official said Wednesday. (published 2106 GMT Wednesday) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI EMERGENCY LAW FAILS TO QUELL CRISIS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The first day of a state of emergency began here Wednesday with raucous street protests and more questions about whether Thailand's army would step in to take control as civil unrest continues to trouble the country. (published 0033 GMT) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par DRAWING A BEAD ON INDIA'S INFLATION TARGET -- HEARD ON THE STREET \f28 \par \par