\par\par \b \uc2 IMF directors say South Korea should keep easy monetary policies\b0\par\par\uc2 363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2014\par 00:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Jan 22 (Reuters) - South Korea should ensure its monetary and fiscal policies support a still-fragile recovery, as Asia's fourth-biggest economy continues to face downside risks to growth, the IMF's executive board said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In their annual assessment of South Korea's economy, many board members at the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  also urged \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be more transparent when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange rate market.\par \par \uc2 South Korean authorities have from time to time been suspected of stepping into the market to smooth excessive volatility in the won.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept interest rates steady and expressed confidence the economic recovery was on track earlier this month. But it faces political pressure for further easing to support the economy.\par \par \uc2 "Domestic demand remains relatively weak and the output gap is expected to remain negative through 2014," the IMF directors said, according to a summary of their discussion released on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The directors "considered it important to ensure that the monetary and fiscal stances remain supportive of the recovery until it is firmly entrenched."\par \par \uc2 The IMF expects South Korea's economy to grow 3.7 percent this year, after expanding 2.8 percent in 2013.\par \par \uc2 IMF board members said South Korea faces downside risks to growth, including severe market stress.\par \par \uc2 Investors are waiting to see how the Federal Reserve's tapering of its bond-buying program this year will affect global financial markets and capital flows in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 South Korea also faces exchange rate pressures due to the slide in the Japanese yen, which could hurt South Korean exporters. The yen's value against the won has fallen by more than 30 percent over the past two years.\par \par \uc2 However, the Korean government has recently refrained from massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. It seeks to support consumption and services and reduce the country's reliance on exports.\par \par \uc2 "Structural policies to bolster domestic demand and continued flexibility of the won in line with fundamentals would facilitate economic rebalancing," IMF directors said. (Reporting by Anna Yukhananov; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-1-25T23:16:52.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140122ea1m00xq2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
