\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2  Won Weakens as Traders Fear  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2014\par 02:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW rises out of the Bollinger downtrend channel negating the appreciation bias for the won and into a higher consolidation range following a sharp overnight rise in the U.S. dollar index. The dollar could now range higher between 1,052 and 1,056 as traders who are short the dollar cover some of their positions on the back of fears of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2 --the won touched a new five-year high against the Japanese yen on Thursday. The South Korea \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  keeps a close watch on the relative value of the won versus the yen and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot market as necessary to ensure price competitiveness for local exporters. USD/KRW is now 1,053.3 from its Thursday closing of 1,050.3. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude-oil futures are slightly higher in early Asian trade Friday after a sharp overnight drop with both the U.S and European oil benchmarks losing around $3/bbl in floor trade. Nymex WTI started the year with its largest one-day dollar and percentage decline since November 2012, while Brent crude fell the most since June 2013. "Although there was some weakness in global equity markets and a stronger U.S. dollar which also prompted some risk-off trade flow on Thursday, we think the signs of further progress in restoring Libyan oil production and exports were the more compelling fundamental story of the day," analyst Tim Evans at Citi Futures says in a note. He says while WTI crude could fall further in the near term, it does have some fundamental support in lower U.S. commercial oil inventories. The EIA inventory report is due later Friday. WTI is up 15 cents at $95.59/bbl, Brent is up 24 cents at $$108.02/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR is struggling in spite of the U.S. dollar index rising sharply overnight. The market suspects \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is capping ringgit weakness at 3.2980, one onshore dealer says. Bank Negara may have placed large orders at that level to cap USD/MYR and this has led traders long the dollar to sell USD/MYR below 3.2980--creating an interim ceiling for the U.S. unit. The Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be trying to prevent the USD/MYR from crossing the major psychological trading barrier at 3.3000--above which stop-loss orders could be triggered sending the ringgit lower. USD/MYR is now 3.2950 from its Thursday closing of 3.2850. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold prices edge steadily higher in early Asian trade with prices extending their overnight gains. "Chinese physical demand for gold remains robust," ANZ says adding that demand rose sharply in the last five days of December. The bank says the \uc2 Shanghai Gold Exchange\uc2  premium rose to $21/oz from $5/oz in December reflecting strong onshore demand. "A potential catalyst for this sharp increase (in demand) is restocking ahead of Chinese New Year in February," it says. Spot gold prices had touched a two-week high on Thursday rising around 2% in the Asian session. "We expect the $1,180-$1,240/oz range to hold in the near-term, and view a flush lower as buying opportunity," ANZ says. Spot gold is at $1,234.46/oz, up $11.46, or 0.9% from its previous close.\par \par \uc2 (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2220 GMT - Australia's S&P/ASX 200 may sag Friday after offshore gains faded, with the S&P 500 ending down 0.9% overnight. BHP (BHP.AU) ADR's fell to US$37.57, suggesting the market heavyweight will open down about 1.6%. Wall Street fell despite lower initial jobless claims and stronger-than-expected U.S. manufacturing ISM data. While some blame falls in China's manufacturing PMI data released Wednesday and Thursday, Australian resources stocks and iron ore prices actually rose in the past two days. Switching from equities to bonds is a possibility, with U.S. 10-year bonds yields down 5 basis points to 2.98% after rising to a 30-month high on Tuesday. Energy stocks may lead declines Friday after Nymex crude oil fell 3% to US$95.44. Expect a quiet end to the week, with no major corporate or economic news scheduled, and a snow storm bearing down on New York. Index last 5367.9. (david.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2054 GMT - There could be "a little bit of weakness to kick off the first trading day of the year" on the NZX-50 given soft offshore leads, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Grant Williamson. He says with no domestic drivers, investors will look offshore for direction but trading will likely remain very light as most market participants are on holiday. The NZX-50 shed 0.7% or 31 points to 4737.01 Tuesday. New Zealand markets were closed Wednesday and Thursday for the New Year's holiday. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 2022 GMT - NZD/USD and AUD/USD both remain fairly well supported despite a stronger USD, but will likely stick to tight ranges, in particular given that Japanese markets remain closed, says Wellington-based OM Financial client advisor Stuart Ive. He notes AUD/USD is benefiting from some USD/JPY selling but "it is all a bit messy as usual." NZD/USD is at 0.8182 early in New Zealand versus 0.8221 late Tuesday ahead of the two-day New Year's holiday while the AUD/USD is at 0.8914 versus 0.8914 late Thursday in Australia. Ive says the Kiwi has support around 0.8175 and resistance at 0.8230 while the Aussie has support at 0.8900 and resistance at 0.8930. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 10:46 EST - Talk about the magnet of 3%. Today offers the latest evidence yields around 3% are attractive to get in. Weakness in US stocks also drive some money back to Treasury bonds. "Investors are coming back into the market after being away from their desks during the holidays, and with cash building up and the 10-year yield above 3%, they found that as a nice buying opportunity," says Larry Milstein, senior trader at R.W. Pressprich & Co. The 10-year note is 4/32 higher, yielding 2.991%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 10:27 EST - Despite a little slippage in the headline number, December ISM manufacturing index looks pretty solid, with the only area of notable weakness showing up in exports. Inventories move into contraction territory, not a bad sign as some concern had arisen that stockpiles were getting excessive. Comments from respondents were almost uniformly positive. "Very, very busy," exclaimed a comment from a furniture & related products manufacturer. "Largest backlog ever," said another in fabricated metal products. Stocks knee-jerk lower. Seems investors are still grappling with taper impact, and how that balances with prospects for better economy. DJIA down 98, S&P 500 off 12. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 10:12 EST - Two factory surveys out today show factories have some momentum going into 2014. What's worth noting is that new orders, as captured in the ISM report, are very strong, suggesting production gains in 2014. But stocks got spooked, sliding to session lows following ISM, though the market has recovered some. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:10 EST - Bond prices have clawed back earlier losses as buyers were lured by highest yields since July 2011. The ISM release is largely in line with expectations and so far the bond market shows muted response. ISM manufacturing index is 57 in December vs 57.3 in November, though a touch better than 56.8 forecast. The 10-year note is flat, yielding 3.008%. The yield falls from a session peak of 3.071%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1453 GMT [Dow Jones]-- ...with only a small \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced today. This 'of course' sends the (wrong) signal that the CBRT will for now accept a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will sell $100 million Thursday to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . SocGen doesn't think the authorities are content to 'let the lira go,' but such 'tiny' amounts will only bolster the perception that they are. USD/TRY now at 2.1831 a whisker below the new record high which is 2.1883. (yeliz.candemir@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 02, 2014 20:40 ET (01:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140103ea130001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
