\par\par \b \uc2 Philippines Peso Gains May Be Fleeting -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2013\par 03:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] The Philippine peso gains 0.2%, reflecting the U.S. dollar's fall due to an improvement in investors' appetite for risk--which results in the safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  being sold. Investor sentiment rose following an upbeat U.S. employment report on Friday that showed more jobs were added in the U.S. economy, bringing the unemployment rate down to 7.0% from 7.3% in the month earlier. The peso, may however, struggle to rally further as the USD/PHP daily chart suggests technical support for the dollar is approaching. The Ichimoku Cloud support zone that begins at 43.88 may stem further decline for the greenback. If the USD/PHP remains above the Cloud by the end of the day, the medium-term potential for dollar upside could continue. The market may have an underlying bullish view for the U.S. dollar over the next few weeks as the drop in the unemployment rate may spur the U.S. Federal Reserve to consider reducing bond purchases sooner. USD/PHP is now 43.93. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Sumco (3436.TO) is down 4.7% at Y914 after the company revised up its FY2013 full-year operating profit forecast to Y17 billion from the previous estimate of Y15 billion. Following the announcement, SMBC Nikko Securities dropped its target price for the firm to Y850 from Y1,000 while maintaining its 2-Neutral rating. "Management raised its operating profit guidance to reflect further cost rationalization and the boost from yen weakness, but demand for 300mm wafers remains lackluster, and it is hard to adopt a more bullish stance," notes SMBC Nikko Securities analyst, Shinobu Takeuchi. Looking ahead, although recovery in demand will depend on sales of end products such as notebook PCs, smartphones and tablet devices and the demand for smaller process sizes, it will be some time before supply-demand tightens for 300mm epitaxial wafers, he says.\par \par \uc2 (yumi.otagaki@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold is slightly lower after a very choppy previous session, as investors digest the implication of a stronger-than-expected U.S. jobs report on the timing of a reduction in the U.S. Federal Reserve's stimulus. The yellow metal may continue to face some downward pressure in the future, given that fund managers continue to shed their long positions in gold. The latest CFTC report shows that on a net basis, net longs in gold dropped for a fifth consecutive week, with positions off by 4,961 lots to 26,774 lots, their lowest level since July 2007. Spot gold is down 0.1% at $1,230/oz. (yue.li@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] China prices are likely to rise next year because of reform measures in spite of a moderation in November, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Ding Shuang says. "The expected reform measures such as interest-rate liberalization could add to inflation pressure next year," Mr. Ding says. He expects on-year CPI growth around 4% at the end of 2014. Producer price index deflation is unlikely to deteriorate on a continued recovery of the world-wide economy, he adds. China's CPI rose 3.0% on year compared with an increase of 3.2% in October; PPI dropped 1.4% on year compared with a 1.5% on-year decline in October. (liyan.qi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW gapped lower at its Monday opening of 1,053 versus its last closing price of 1,058, and fell to as low as 1,052 as the market reacted to the overnight drop in the dollar index. The won gained as much as 0.6% versus the greenback and notched a new 5-year high of 10.1997 to the Japanese yen. The greenback fell overnight as investors turned more optimistic on risky assets, shrugging off the scepter of the U.S. Federal Reserve reducing bond-buying, on the back of strong U.S. job data released Friday. Further gains for the South Korea won might be limited by the base of the daily USD/KRW Bollinger downtrend channel at 1,052 and by the possibility that \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap won strength. The won is now at its strongest level against the Japanese yen since 2008--a situation that might warrant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to preserve the price competitiveness that South Korea's exporters have over Japanese rivals due to the relatively cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/KRW is now 1,053.0. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY falls sharply with the yuan hitting a fresh high after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the yuan fixing at an record high level for the second consecutive day. The pair is at 6.0729 after hitting 6.0715 onshore, vs. 6.0817 Friday's close. The PBOC set the central parity at 6.1130 vs. 6.1232 Friday. The stronger yuan came after China posted its biggest trade surplus in almost five years. "I think this round will be like the yuan's rally in October when the PBOC suddenly encouraged more yuan strength after letting the yuan consolidate for a long time," says a Beijing-based trader. The yuan's last record level was at 6.0802 in late October. He says he expects the pair to have a support at 6.05 before the year-end. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.1184/6.1227 from 6.1320/6.1335 late Friday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite months of broker chatter, going long on North Asian stocks isn't as much of a consensus trade as some investors might think, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says. As proof, it points to recent data showing emerging markets and Asia-focused funds still have strong underweights in Korean and Taiwanese stocks, while remaining overweight on Southeast Asia. Goldman--which late last month upgraded Chinese stocks to overweight--says it sees potential for more funds to flow into the region, which "offer the clearest way to gain exposure to improving global economic growth," in the firm's view. (mia.lamar@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] The baht is a tad stronger as the possibility of an end to political tensions appears. Prime Minister Yingluck Shinawatra has requested royal approval to dissolve parliament to hold a referendum which could decide her fate as leader of the government. The baht rose to 32.07 from its Friday closing price of 32.22, implying a nearly 0.5% gain for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar. Further gains for the baht appears possible, charts suggest. USD/THB may fall to the base of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 31.88 before finding support. The overall weakness of the U.S. unit, following a boost in investor confidence triggered by a strong U.S. employment data on Friday, may also add to the short-term bearish dollar view. USD/THB is now 32.07. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] IMM commitment of traders report shows net JPY short non-commercial positions vs. USD rose to 133,383 contracts in the week to Dec 3 vs. 123,202 contracts in the week earlier. This shows that commodity trading advisers and hedge funds increased the bets against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid increased speculation for Fed's tapering and continued \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  easing. Short-term speculators may further add to anti-JPY wagers after encouraging U.S. jobs data bolstered the case for an early start of U.S. tapering. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] China inflation came in lower than expected in November with the consumer price index up 3.0% on year compared with 3.2% in October. That signals that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent tighter stance is gaining traction though moderating food prices are also part of the reason. Food prices were down 0.2% on month. Meanwhile deflation in the producer price index showed signs of easing with a 1.4% on-year drop. Month-to-month the index held steady, the fourth straight month without a fall.(richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude-oil futures are in positive territory in early Asian trade Monday after finishing higher last week. Markets are still digesting Friday's positive U.S. jobs data, and subsequent concerns about early tapering of asset purchases by the U.S. Federal Reserve. The data "support a better U.S. economic outlook and therefore energy markets, but we didn't see any immediate impact on prices," ANZ Research says in a note. "Potential North Sea production cuts due to severe weather in Europe could see Brent perform better this week," it says. Money managers boosted their bets that crude oil prices would rise to their highest level in six weeks, CFTC data for the week ended Dec. 3 showed. China's November trade data showed a mild recovery from seasonal lows, with crude imports recovering 19% on month. Chinese consumer price and industrial production data are also expected today. Nymex crude is up 17 cents at $97.82/bbl, Brent is up 1 cent at $111.62/bbl. (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] The Chinese yuan jumps almost 0.2% higher after the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  set the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a new record benchmark rate against the U.S. dollar. The move follows an overnight decline in the ICE dollar index, which tracks the greenback's strength against six other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar fell late Friday after a sterling jobs report boosted investors' confidence, who sold the safe haven asset for riskier, higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yuan's gains add to optimism for China investors after data published Sunday revealed a 12.7% rise in exports on a yearly basis and the biggest trade surplus in almost five years. Domestic consumption, reflected in the import data, was relatively weak, which may be another reason the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to encourage more locals to spend. USD/CNY is now 6.0729 versus its Friday closing price of 6.0817. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 08, 2013 21:29 ET (02:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131209e9c90007j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
