\par\par \b \uc2 USD/Asia Slides on Improved Investor Sentiment -- Asia Daily  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 3,541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2013\par 01:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in Asia today: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY   6.0802-6.0864   6.0745-6.0900 \par USD/TWD   29.550-29.650   29.500-29.710 \par USD/KRW    1,052-1,057     1,050-1,060 \par USD/SGD   1.2500-1.2538   1.2466-1.2574 \par USD/MYR   3.2115-3.2280   3.2000-3.2430 \par USD/THB    32.20-32.28     32.00-32.37 \par USD/PHP    43.80-43.88     43.69-43.98 \par USD/IDR   11,800-12,000   11,750-12,000 \par USD/INR    61.44-61.72     61.00-62.00 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/CNY--downside bias. A lower PBOC dollar/yuan parity rate is to be expected, possibly to set a new record low after Friday's 6.1232. The dollar index fell sharply late Friday despite strong U.S. jobs data that suggests a reduction in bond-buying by the U.S. Federal Reserve might be coming. Spot USD/CNY has dropped into the Bollinger downtrend channel after the low USD/CNY parity rate Friday, and may continue lower, capped by the channel's ceiling at 6.0864. China's exports grew 12.7% in November on a yearly basis, and the country's trade surplus rose to its highest in nearly five years, which ought to diffuse concerns of a 'hard landing' for the Chinese economy. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 6.0802 (record low), then at 6.0800 (psychological support), before 6.0745 (base of Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 6.0864 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 6.0900 (psychological resistance), before 6.0957 (daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/TWD--consolidation lower. USD/TWD might drop below the 20-day Bollinger mid at 26.560 as the dollar index fell overnight, pulling most USD/Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower Monday. The dollar is weaker as investors' appetite for risky assets increases following the strong growth in U.S. jobs data released Friday. Traders may however buy the dollar on dips as the upbeat U.S. employment data also raises the possibility that the U.S. Federal Reserve might sooner than expected reduce bond-buying. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 29.560 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 29.500 (psychological support), before 29.470 (base of daily Ichimoku Cloud). Immediate resistance is likely at 29.650 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 29.710 (top of daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone), before 29.730 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW--consolidation lower. USD/KRW gaps lower at the opening of 1,053 versus its last closing price of 1,058 as the market reacts to the drop in the dollar index. The greenback fell overnight as investors turned more optimistic on risky assets, shrugging off the scepter of the U.S. Federal Reserve reducing bond-buying, on the back of very strong U.S. job data released Friday. Further gains for the South Korea won might be limited by the base of the daily Bollinger downtrend channel, which has come into effect due to the sharp fall of the dollar, and by the possibility that \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap won strength. The won is now at a fresh five-year high to the Japanese yen--a situation that might warrant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to preserve the price competitiveness that South Korea's exporters have over Japanese rivals due to the relatively cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,052 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 1,050 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 1,057 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 1,060 (psychological resistance), before 1,062 (20-day Bollinger mid-resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/SGD--consolidation lower. USD/SGD is looking slightly weaker after it dropped below the 20-day Bollinger mid support in reaction to the very strong U.S. jobs report released Friday that has boosted investor sentiment. The positive data has resulted in a selling of the safe haven U.S. dollar for higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as those in Asia. Though the strong growth in U.S. employment could convince the U.S. Federal Reserve to reduce bond-buying sooner, it appears that investors are putting aside those fears for now. USD/SGD may consolidate lower toward the entrance of the daily Bollinger downtrend channel at 1.2466, but buying-on-dips might stall further declines. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2500 (psychological support), before 1.2466 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 1.2502 (20-day Bollinger mid resistance), then at 1.2538 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 1.2574 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/MYR--consolidation lower. USD/MYR may linger in a range of 3.2115-3.2280 after the U.S. dollar selloff late Friday thwarted bullish USD/MYR technical signals that appeared a day earlier. The rise of investor confidence owing to the upbeat U.S. jobs data on Friday has given high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  a boost and sent the dollar lower, despite connotations that the U.S. Federal Reserve could reduce bond-buying soon due to the drop in the unemployment rate. USD/MYR may even take on a mild bearish bias if it ends Monday below the 20-day Bollinger mid support at 3.2115. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.2115 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 3.2000 (psychological support), before 3.1950 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 3.2280 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel and top of Ichimoku Cloud resistance), before 3.2430 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/THB--consolidation. Despite an overall weaker U.S. dollar, USD/THB may remain elevated as domestic political tensions come to a standoff. Thai protesters have issued a Monday deadline for incumbent Prime Minister Yingluck to step down. The mass resignation of opposition leaders in the government has added pressure to situation, which is likely to affect the baht. Prime Minister Yingluck has proposed a referendum to quell the unrest, but said that the protesters must be prepared to accept the result of the poll. Until the political noise dies down, the baht will likely remain weak. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 32.20 (psychological support), then at 32.00 (psychological support), before 31.80 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 32.28 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 32.37 (3-month high), before 32.56 (top of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/PHP--consolidation lower. USD/PHP may drop back into the consolidation range within the daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone as investors dump the U.S. dollar for higher-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  following a surge in investor confidence triggered by strong U.S. employment data on Friday. USD/PHP could however retain a bullish bias as long as it stays above the base of the Bollinger uptrend channel that now supports at 43.69. The strong U.S. data may also motivate buying of the dollar on dips, as it raises the possibility of a sooner-than-expected U.S. Federal Reserve reduction in bond-buying. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 43.80 (psychological support), then at 43.69 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 43.50 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is likely at 43.88 (top of Ichimoku Cloud), then at 43.98 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 44.00 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/IDR--consolidation lower. USD/IDR could ease from its recent 5-year high as the dollar slips broadly on investors' improved appetite for risky assets, such as the rupiah. The rebound in investor confidence was motivated by strong U.S. jobs growth revealed on Friday. The upbeat data is helping stocks to rally in Asia, but could also increase chances that the U.S. Federal Reserve may reduce bond-buying sooner--which could result in a bounce for the U.S. dollar. Thus, the greenback may not fall far before traders take the opportunity to bet on an earlier reduction in bond-buying by the Fed. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 11,800 (psychological support), then at 11,750 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 11,510 (20-day Bollinger mid support). Immediate resistance is at 12,000 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel and psychological resistance and possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level), before 12,200 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/INR--downside bias. USD/INR may drop into the daily Bollinger downtrend channel that begins at 61.44 and hence give the rupee more momentum to gain. The upbeat U.S non-farm payrolls report released on Friday has resulted in a weaker U.S. dollar as investors dump the safe haven asset for riskier investments, even if the data increases chances of earlier reduction in bond buying by the U.S. Federal Reserve. The rupee has been looking more attractive after the gains made by the opposition in the state elections. This bodes well for the more business-friendly opposition party, and therefore for the rupee, as the market looks further ahead to general elections in May 2014. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 61.44 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 61.00 (psychological support), before 60.82 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 61.72 (daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone), then at 62.00 (psychological resistance), before 62.05 (20-day Bollinger mid resistance).\par \par \uc2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 8 Dec 2013 19:59 EDT  USD/Asia Slides on Improved Investor Sentiment -- -2-\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 9 Dec 2013 18:44 EDT  USD/JPY Up on Weak Yen, Positive Risk Sentiment -- Asia Daily \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Outlook\par \par \uc2 The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY   102.89-103.38    102.25-103.74 \par EUR/USD   1.3695-1.3747    1.3627-1.3787 \par AUD/USD   0.9068-0.9135    0.8988-0.9146 \par NZD/USD   0.8252-0.8320    0.8139-0.8393 \par GBP/USD   1.6321-1.6442    1.6296-1.6455 \par USD/CHF   0.8888-0.8934    0.8865-0.8983 \par USD/CAD   1.0618-1.0669    1.0600-1.0707 \par EUR/JPY   141.00-142.00    138.91-143.00 \par EUR/GBP   0.8356-0.8396    0.8341-0.8414 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/JPY--to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting four-day high 103.35 Monday. Underpinned by negative yen sentiment as weaker-than-expected Japan 3Q GDP growth (+1.1% versus preliminary +1.9% reading) and surprise October current account deficit bolster expectations that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  will expand monetary easing measures further to support economic growth and to reach its 2% inflation target by the end of March 2016. USD/JPY also supported by demand from Japan importers and investment trusts; yen-funded carry trades amid positive investor risk appetite (S&P rose 0.18% overnight) as outlook for global economy improved after Friday's robust U.S. November non-farm payrolls report and Sunday's strong China November trade data; and investors grew comfortable with increased prospect of a potential reduction in the Federal Reserve's $85-billion-a-month bond-buying program at the Fed's next policy meeting on Dec. 17-18 - St. Louis Fed President James Bullard said Monday that recent improvement in the U.S. job market boosted the case for tapering, while Richmond Fed President Jeffrey Lacker and Dallas Fed President Richard Fisher both said it is time for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to begin winding down the stimulus. But USD/JPY gains tempered by Japan exporter sales. Yen crosses vulnerable to 0530 GMT China November retail sales, fixed assets investment, industrial output data. Data focus: 2350 GMT Japan October tertiary industry index, November money stock, 4Q business outlook survey; 0500 GMT Japan November consumer confidence survey; 0600 GMT Japan November preliminary machine tool orders; 1230 GMT U.S. November NFIB Index of Small Business Optimism; 1500 GMT U.S. October Monthly wholesale trade. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, 5- & 15-day moving averages advancing. Resistance at 103.35-103.38 (Monday's high-Dec. 3 high); breach would expose upside to 103.74 (four-and-a-half year high when hit on May 22), then 105.49 (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of decline from June 22, 2007 high of 124.14 to Oct. 31, 2011 low of 75.32). Support at 102.89 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 102.25 (hourly chart), then 101.62 (Thursday's low) and 101.14 (Nov. 25 low).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EUR/USD--to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting five-week high 1.3747 Monday. Supported by euro demand on EUR/JPY cross amid positive risk appetite and weak yen sentiment; European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Draghi's signal Thursday that the ECB doesn't have any immediate plans to ease policy further. But euro sentiment dented by surprise 1.2% on-month drop in Germany October industrial production index (defying forecast for 0.8% rise). EUR/USD gains also tempered by euro sales on retreating EUR/GBP cross. Data & event focus: 0700 GMT Germany October manufacturing turnover, 1200 GMT ECB President Mario Draghi speech. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought; 5- & 15-day moving averages advancing. Resistance at 1.3747 (Monday's high); breach expose upside to 1.3787 (Oct. 30 high), then 1.3833 (Oct. 25 swing high). Support at 1.3695 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.3627 (Friday's low), then 1.3542 (Thursday's low).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AUD/USD--to range-range. Supported by strong November trade data out of China, Australia's biggest trading partner; Aussie demand on AUD/JPY cross amid positive risk appetite and weak yen sentiment; buoyant commodity prices. But AUD/USD gains tempered by official rhetoric against Aussie strength. Data focus: 0030 GMT Australia October housing finance approvals, November NAB business survey. Daily chart tilting positive as stochastics rising from oversold, MACD staging bullish crossover against its exponential moving average. Resistance at 0.9135 (Monday's high), then at 0.9146 (Dec. 3 high); breach would target 0.9168 (Dec. 2 high), then 0.9204 (Nov. 26 high) and 0.9249 (Nov. 22 high). Support at 0.9068 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.8988 (Friday's low), then 0.8921 (Sept. 2 low), and 0.8887 (Aug. 30 reaction low).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NZD/USD--to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting two-week high 0.8320 Monday. Supported by Kiwi demand on NZD/JPY cross amid positive risk appetite and weak yen sentiment; buoyant commodity prices. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish; five-day moving average above 15-day MA and advancing. Resistance at 0.8320 (Monday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8393 (Nov. 20 high), then 0.8405 (Nov. 18 high). Support at 0.8252 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.8139 (Friday's low), then 0.8081 (Nov. 29 reaction low).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GBP/USD--to trade in higher range. Supported by sterling demand on GBP/JPY cross amid positive risk appetite and weak yen sentiment; sterling demand on retreating EUR/GBP cross; expectations that the Bank of England may have to raise interest rates sooner than previously expected after recent raft of upbeat U.K. data. Data focus: 0001 GMT U.K. November RICS residential market survey; 0930 GMT U.K. October industrial production & manufacturing output, October global goods trade balance; 1500 GMT U.K. November NIESR monthly GDP estimates. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought; 5- & 15-day moving averages advancing. Resistance at 1.6432-1.6442 band (Monday's high-Dec. 2 two-year high); breach would target 1.6455 (Aug. 29, 2011 high), then 1.6570 (Aug. 23, 2011 high). Support at 1.6321 (Monday's low), then at 1.6296 (Friday's low); breach would target 1.6275 (Nov. 28 low), then 1.6195 (Nov. 27 low) and 1.6137-1.6131 band (Nov. 26 low-Nov. 25 low).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/CHF--to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting six-week low 0.8893 Monday. Undermined by broadly weaker dollar undertone; franc demand on soft EUR/CHF cross, and on buoyant CHF/JPY cross amid weak yen sentiment. But CHF sentiment dented by rise in Switzerland jobless rate to 3.2% in November from 3.1% in October. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold; five- & 15-day moving averages declining. Support at 0.8893-0.8888 (Monday's low-Oct. 24 reaction low); breach would target 0.8865 (Nov. 7, 2011 low), then 0.8758 (Nov. 3, 2011 low) and 0.8566 (Oct. 27, 2011 reaction low). Resistance at 0.8934 (Monday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8983 (Friday's high), then 0.9048 (Thursday's high).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/CAD--to trade in lower range. Undermined by loonie demand on CAD/JPY cross amid positive risk appetite and weak yen sentiment. But loonie sentiment dented by bigger-than-expected 3.0% on-month drop in Canada November housing starts to 192,235 in November (versus 200,000 forecast). USD/CAD losses also tempered by weaker oil prices (Nymex crude settled 31 cents lower Monday at $97.34/bbl). Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics falling from overbought. Support at 1.0618 (Friday's low), then at 1.0600 (Dec. 2 low); breach would target 1.0555 (Nov. 29 low), then 1.0527 (Nov. 27 low) and 1.0517-1.0509 band (Nov. 26 low-Nov. 22 low). Resistance at 1.0669 (Monday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0707 (Wednesday's high), then 1.0851 (May 25, 2010 high), 1.0869 (Nov. 2, 2009 reaction high) and 1.0992 (Sept. 28, 2009 reaction high).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EUR/JPY--to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting five-year high 141.95 Monday. Underpinned by positive risk appetite; weak yen sentiment; demand from Japan importers. But EUR/JPY gains tempered by Japan exporter sales. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought; 5- & 15-day moving averages advancing. Resistance at 141.95-142.00 (Monday's high-psychological level); breach would expose upside to psychological round-numbered levels of 143.00 up to 152.00, then 152.07 (76.4% Fibonacci retracement of 169.97-94.12 July 23, 2008-July 24, 2012 decline). Support at 141.00 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 138.91 (Friday's low), then 138.40 (Wednesday's low).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EUR/GBP--to trade in lower range. Daily chart still positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish; five-day moving average rising above 15-day MA. Support at 0.8356 (Monday's low); breach would target 0.8341 (Friday's low), then 0.8288 (Thursday's low) and 0.8270 (Wednesday's low). Resistance at 0.8396 (Monday's high); breach would target 0.8414 (Nov. 20 high), then 0.8463-0.8469 band (Nov. 13 high-Nov. 5 high).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Jerry Tan at jerry.tan@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors which should determine any user's investment decisions. Nothing contained in this service constitutes personalized investment advice. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors shall not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article. This article does not constitute or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 09, 2013 18:44 ET (23:44 GMT)\f28 \p