\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA MARKETS: Shanghai Stocks Extend Losses; Yen Weakens\b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Inman   \par \uc2 460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2013\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Asian markets were mostly lower Tuesday, with the Australian dollar dipping after the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  kept rates at a record low, while Japanese stocks got a boost from a weaker yen.\par \par \uc2 Asia was given a negative lead from the U.S., where Wall Street closed lower after reports showing a pickup in the manufacturing sector increased fears that the Federal Reserve could start to roll back its bond-buying program. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stimulus has been a significant driver of markets this year.\par \par \uc2 The major piece of data to come out of the U.S. this week will be the monthly labor report on Friday, a much-watched indicator that has been used to guess the Fed's monetary policy plans.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar pushed higher against the yen overnight and consolidated in Asian trade. The dollar gained 0.5% on Monday and traded at Yen103.19 Monday. Softness in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the Nikkei gain ground, with the index up 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 Yen weakness in recent weeks has helped revive the Japanese stock market, which experienced rangebound trading for several months after crashing heavily earlier in the summer. With the market up 51.6% so far this year, the Tokyo stock rally is back on track.\par \par \uc2 Chinese shares extended Monday's selloff as investors continued to fret about oversupply of new shares ahead of an upcoming restart of the country's initial public offering market. The local securities regulator on Saturday released guidelines for a revamp of the country's IPO system, which could mean the end to a 13-month hiatus in domestic listings.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite lost 0.1%, adding to a 0.6% decline on Monday. The growth enterprise board in Shenzhen, which lost 8.3% in the previous session, fell another 1.4% on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    In Hong Kong, the Hang Seng Index   lost 0.6%. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was down 0.4% after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  kept rates at a low of 2.5%, meeting expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar dipped as low as 90.66 U.S. cents from 90.89 U.S. cents after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said that "a lower level of the exchange rate is likely to be needed to achieve balanced growth in the economy." The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been bumped around in recent weeks by remarks from officials at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over whether it was likely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere in the region, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Kospi fell 0.8% and Singapore's Straits Times Index was up less than 0.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 03, 2013 02:32 ET (07:32 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131203e9c3000lw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
