\par\par \b \uc2 Vietnam Banks Hold USD/VND Rates Despite Low Liquidity -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,824 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2013\par 03:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnamese banks led by \uc2 Vietcombank\uc2  (VCB.VH) are holding their USD/VND rates at 21,075-21,115 Friday, unchanged from Thursday, in an effort to help the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buy more dollars at low costs, despite low liquidity in the local interbank market, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say. For the week ended Nov. 8, the average daily dollar-dong transaction volume in the interbank market was $443 million, down from $665 million a week earlier, according to data released by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday. "Local commercial banks are trying to keep their rates little-changed because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to buy as much dollars at low cost as possible," says a dealer with a joint stock commercial bank from Hanoi. Gold shops have raised their rates because dollar supply is thin. Private dealers with gold shops say they expect the exchange rate in the free market to move in a VND21,220-VND21,240 range over the weekend and higher next week. (phammuoi.nguyen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] Sharp (6753.TO) is up 8.1% at Y321 after a Nikkei report that the LCD panel maker is set to begin production of small- and mid-size LCD panels at its Kameyama No. 2 factory in order to supply to China's \uc2 ZTE Corp\uc2 . "How to raise and maintain high operating rates at its factories has been a challenge for Sharp," says Naoki Fujiwara, fund manager at Shinkin Asset Management. "Hence, the shares has responded positively to the report," he says. Although Sharps' smaller LCD panels business moved into the black at the operating line in 2Q, after losses through 1Q, efforts to increase the production weighting of smaller LCDs at the Kameyama No. 2 plant appear to be running around one fiscal quarter behind plan, notes Mika Nishimura, analyst at Mitsubishi UFJ \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  Securities.(yumi.otagaki@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0159 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/SGD, which entered the Bollinger uptrend channel Thursday, has stopped in its trek higher upon hitting the Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone at 1.2495. Further strength for the greenback depends on the USD/SGD closing Friday above this mark. Failure to do so could result in a pullback and the Bollinger uptrend channel being threatened. If the USD/SGD closes above 1.2495 today, its next target could be the weekly Bollinger mid resistance line at 1.2572. Data released Thursday showed a stronger-than-forecast rise in Singapore's 3Q Gross Domestic Product, but it had little impact on the Singapore dollar, which still reacts more to external factors such as the U.S. Federal Reserve potentially reducing monetary stimulus in months to come. The USD/SGD is now 1.2493. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] The South Korea won is slightly stronger Friday but the dollar may later take on a more bullish bias if it ends Friday above KRW1,063 - the 20-day Bollinger mid that now acts as a technical resistance. Another factor which could spur USD/KRW higher is the steep rise of USD/JPY overnight. Yen weakness is typically followed by weakness for the the \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  won as \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market to ensure that the won retains its relative value to the yen - which gives South Korea exporters a competitive advantage. The USD/KRW has been trading with a firm tone ahead of the 1,060 psychological support, but has struggled to rally for now due to exporters selling near 1,063, said a trader. The USD/KRW is now 1,061.8. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Japan Tobacco\uc2  (2914.TO) or JT is down 3.5% at Y3,590 after The Yomiuri Shimbun report that the government has decided against raising tobacco taxes in April when a sales tax hike kicks in. The newspaper says multiple members in the government's Research Commission on the Tax System said tobacco taxes increase in 2014 will be impossible as an increase in tobacco taxes amid a sales tax hike could lead to a decline in smokers, which could drastically reduce tax revenue. "The market appears to be concerned that the government might sell its JT shares to make up for lost tax revenue a result of an abandoned tobacco tax hike. That will deteriorate supply and demand conditions of the JT shares," says Naoki Fujiwara, fund manager at Shinkin Asset Management. (yumi.otagaki@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold is in a tight range as investors remain cautious about the prospects of the metal amid concerns about the U.S. Federal Reserve winding down its gold-supportive stimulus program, as the economic growth picks up pace. "People are finding it hard to find a reason to own gold," says Adam Klopfenstein, a senior market strategist with Archer Financial Services. "Demand for gold as a store of value is not going to be there when inflation is low," he says. Besides, upbeat U.S. manufacturing purchasing managers' index that rose to 54.3 in November compared with 51.8 in October, could underscore concerns about the Fed tapering its monetary stimulus program in coming months. Spot gold is at $1,243.50/oz, up $1.10 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0125 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares open up 0.4% at 8127.68 tracking the overnight rally on Wall Street but the upward momentum is likely to lose steam soon as most foreign investors are still pulling out of emerging markets due to lingering uncertainties and a lack of cues, says a local trader who tips a 8100-8250 band. "The lackluster 4Q guidance by various major tech players is also weighing on investors," he notes. Taiwan PC maker Acer (2353.TW) opens up 4.5% at NT$16.30 following news its founder Stan Shih will return as the company's chairman. "The rise is likely to be short-lived because the biggest problem faced by Taiwan's PC industry is lack of innovation against the rise of hand-held devices," the trader adds. Tech names are mostly up; Hon Hai (2317.TW) is up 0.9% at NT$75.70, HTC (2417.TW) gains 0.6% at NT$156.50 while TSMC (2330.TW) is flat at NT$101.00 (jenny.hsu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0022 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals may continue to move sideways with move on the downside likely to be limited on an upbeat U.S. manufacturing purchasing managers' index which rose to 54.3 in November, according to a preliminary reading, compared with 51.8 in October. Analysts say that the data should be able to support metal prices; the U.S. is the world's second biggest consumer of metals after China. LME 3-month copper ended 0.3% higher at $7,020/ton, while zinc fell 0.1% to $1,888/ton. "Copper bounces, but the larger trend in base metals remains bearish," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says in a report based on technical indicators. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0010 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian shares are rallying early, surging after Aussie dollar-dampening comments from the country's top central banker and strong gains overnight on Wall Street. The benchmark S&P/ASX 200 is up 1.2% at 5352.6, with broad gains across every sector. Tim Radford, an analyst at Rivkin Securities, says that with ex-dividend selling of Australia's big four banks now easing, pressure on the wider market will lift and allow for higher prices in the next few sessions. "We expect buying momentum to return, pushing the ASX 200 back toward 52-week highs around 5,400 points," he says. ANZ (ANZ.AU), Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU), \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  (NAB.AU) and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) are between 1.1% and 1.3% higher. Large mining companies are also stronger, with \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  (BHP.AU) and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) both up 0.9%. Ric Spooner, chief market analyst at CMC Markets, says comments late Thursday from Reserve Bank Governor Glenn Stevens that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was open to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on an overly strong Aussie was "perfectly timed" jawboning that has weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The relative strength of the Aussie has dented export-driven sectors of the economy and added to local costs. (robb.stewart@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD got a lift after RBNZ Assistant Governor John McDermott said it was overvalued but that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market would have little impact. "Whilst the bulk of his speech was the usual rhetoric about the overvalued Kiwi, it looks like the market has dismissed it off hand as jawboning and instead focused on his comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the futility of it, says Chris Hunter, corporate dealing manager at \uc2 Western Union\uc2  Business Solutions. The pair is at 0.8225 versus 0.8204 prior to McDermott's speech in which he noted daily foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions in New Zealand dollars "swamp any practical \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  capacity," as they average around NZ$100 billion a day with more than 80% of transactions taking place offshore. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2351 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude is likely to trade with a bullish bias near-term after hitting a three-week high of $95.63/bbl Thursday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is positive-biased as the MACD indicator is bullish, the slow stochastic measure is rising from the oversold level, while the five-day moving average looks poised to stage a bullish crossover against the 15-day moving average. A rise above the $95.63 resistance would expose the upside to $96.65 (Nov. 1 high), then to $97.03 (Oct. 31 high), $97.82 (Oct. 30 high) and $98.57 (Oct. 29 high, near the 200-day moving average). But a drop below $93.47 (Thursday's low) would temper the positive near-term view, targeting $93.25 (Wednesday's low), then $92.43 (Tuesday's reaction low), $91.26 (June 3 reaction low), $90.11 (May 1 reaction low) and the psychological $90.00 line. January crude is down 19 cents at $95.25 on Globex. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2348 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at six foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 3.4 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders. The eighth consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for the market at the open, although the yen value basis of the figures is unknown. Buy orders total 14.7 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 11.3 million shares. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 21, 2013 21:14 ET (02:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131122e9bm0005e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
