\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea Oct  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves rise to record on suspected  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 263 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2013\par 21:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Nov 5 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves climbed to a record in October, setting a new high for the fourth straight month and jumping by the biggest amount seen since October 2011, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Tuesday. South Korea's foreign reserves stood at $343.23 billion at the end of October, up $6.3 billion from the end of September on management gains and as \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the euro rose in value against the U.S. dollar, \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  said. The data comes after market participants suspected government \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the won on several occasions last month. South Korean policymakers have repeatedly warned that the won's recent gains are excessive. It gained 1.2 percent against the dollar last month on continued foreign inflows on the country's improved fundamentals and global economic standing.\par \par \uc2  The authorities engage in market-smoothing measures to decrease volatility in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but do not provide details of their actions nor do they confirm them. South Korea had the seventh largest foreign reserves as of the end of September, 90.5 percent of which were in the form of securities.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     \par      South Korean foreign reserves (in $ bln, at end-month):\par  Oct      Sep      Aug      Jul      Jun      May      Apr\par  343.23   336.92   331.09   329.71   326.44   328.10   328.80\par \f2