\par\par \b \uc2 USD/KRW Spikes To 1,062 On Fear Of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2013\par 07:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0545 GMT [Dow Jones] The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won slumped rapidly on fear of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , after \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it is monitoring the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, concerned about volatility, and will take action if necessary. This form of verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , construed by market participants as the BOK's warning to punters to avoid speculating on the won, sent USD/KRW soaring to 1,062 from 1,055 within minutes in a wave of panicked USD-short covering. The move implies a 0.7% depreciation of the won. The USD/KRW has now settled slightly lower at 1,059.0. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0515 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD has "popped higher" following a narrower than expected local trade deficit and upbeat Chinese data, although much of the gains have now disappeared as focus returns to the USD story, says \uc2 HiFX\uc2  Head of Trading Alex Hill. The pair is at 0.8412 vs. 0.8377 early this morning. Statistics NZ earlier said the September trade deficit was just NZ$199 million vs. market expectation of a NZ$800 million deficit, while the initial October HSBC China's manufacturing activity came out at 50.9 vs. a final reading of 50.2 in September. Hill puts support for the pair at 0.8380 with resistance coming at 0.8520. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0507 GMT [Dow Jones] As the weekend approaches, certainty is rising for a medium-term bearish signal for USD/SGD on the weekly chart. On a Friday close below 1.2380, the USD/SGD would be beneath the Ichimoku Cloud support zone and also inside the Bollinger downtrend channel. The significance of the dollar falling below the Cloud support is increased by the Chikou line--a momentum indicator that is current price action overlayed 26 periods backwards--also dropping below the Cloud support. The additional bearish technical indicator of the Bollinger downtrend channel, which has already been in play since 5 weeks ago, adds to conviction that USD/SGD could fall to its May low of 1.2261 in weeks to come. USD/SGD is now 1.2368. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] Though today's U.S. intial jobless claims report will be tainted by effects of the two-week partial government shutdown, it may still create volatility in markets when it is released later. Economists will attempt to strip out the effects of claims by government workers, and then arrive at a more accurate private sector unemployment picture. Steady job creation is one of the main variables which will influence when the Federal Reserve tapers its monetary stimulus - a key event risk to financial markets. The week before, U.S. intial jobless claims dropped 15,000 to 358,000. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0340 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered revises up its forecast for China's gross domestic product growth for 2013 to 7.6% from 7.5%. It revises its forecast for 2014 to 7.4% from 7.2%. A number of indicators suggests an improvement in broad-based growth in the third quarter and momentum should continue, it says in a research note. Economic growth expanded 7.8% on year in the third quarter--up from 7.5% in the second quarter. Standard Chartered raises its CPI forecast to 2.7% for this year from 2.5% and to 3.3% for 2014 from 3.0%. (grace.zhu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0339 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are lower midday, extending Wednesday's losses, as investors continue to desert cyclical shares amid uncertainties over economic outlook, analysts say. The Shanghai Composite Index falls 0.2% to 2177.90 and may find its immediate support at 2150. "The chances for a strong market after the conclusion of a key party meeting in November do not look very good," HFT Investment Management says in a research note, citing that the domestic economy is unlikely to post a marked rise in 4Q due to high base effect. Among actives, cyclical shares are lower with Yanzhou Coal Mining (600188.SH) down 2.3% at CNY10.99, metal firm Chalco (601600.SH) off 2.1% at CNY4.18 and \uc2 Baoshan Iron & Steel\uc2  (600019.SH) losing 1.2% to CNY4.11. The Shenzhen Composite Index is flat at 1065.34. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0311 GMT [Dow Jones] The market has doubts about Beijing's plan to increase the supply of homes and cool prices. Implementation and poor quality top a list of concerns. The city will release land for 20,000 units this year and 50,000 next. They will 30% cheaper than existing homes in the city and can't be resold within five years of purchase and even then will attract a 30% capital-gains tax. Similar lower-cost, price-controlled projects have been accumulated by corrupt officials. Developers show little interest in building them and construction quality is substandard, the state-run China Securities Journal says. (esther.fung@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0253 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY falls to another fresh low despite the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to guide the pair's daily fixing slightly higher following the dollar's overnight strength against its major rivals. "We're kind of clueless now as to where the pair may go north further because trading tends to be rangebound in the absence of fresh catalysts, which is where we are now," says a Beijing-based local bank trader. The fact that such a low reading of the pair hasn't attracted a bout of dollar buying indicates market players' uncertain outlook on the dollar/yuan movement going forward, the trader adds. The USD/CNY is at 6.0816 vs. 6.0835 late Wednesday, while the central parity is set at 6.1335. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are up at 6.1495/6.1535 from 6.1490/6.1515 late Wednesday. (yue.li@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0250 GMT [Dow Jones] Oil prices edged higher with ICE Brent crude moving into positive territory after preliminary manufacturing data from China touched a seven-month high. The preliminary HSBC China Manufacturing PMI, a gauge of nationwide manufacturing activity, rose to 50.9 in October, compared with a final reading of 50.2 in September. A figure above 50 indicates expansion, while a reading below 50 indicates contraction. The PMI data is "encouraging" and "denotes continuous expansion in economic activities in China," analyst Chee Tat at Phillip Futures says. "This will buoy demand for crude oil, which has a positive impact on crude oil prices," he says, adding that he's looking forward to the release of the official and private China manufacturing PMI for more indications regarding the Chinese economy. Nymex WTI crude is up 60 cents at $97.44/bbl, ICE Brent crude is up 10 cents at $107.90/bbl. (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] China's economy may surprise on the upside in the fourth quarter, judging from a preliminary reading of the \uc2 HSBC\uc2  manufacturing purchasing managers' index, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  economist Ken Peng. "It looks like we're going to get a few more months of better-than-expected production," says Mr. Peng. "You're seeing a solid industrial response to export demand." But he added that he would wait for the final reading of the \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI, as well as the competing official index, before drawing any firm conclusions. "The flash PMI lost some credibility last month, when there was a big swing" between preliminary and final figures, he noted. The preliminary or "flash" reading of the \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI came in at 50.9 in October, up from a final reading of 50.2 the previous month. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] An uptick in the HSBC China manufacturing purchasing managers index flash reading for October signals economic weakness the previous month was partly caused by nothing more serious than a run of national holidays, says \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  economist Zhou Hao. The flash, or preliminary reading of the PMI, picked up to 50.9 in October from a final reading of 50.2 in September. Mr. Zhou also thinks weakness in exports, which fell 0.3% on-year in September, can be put down to the holiday effect and predicts a return to export growth of about 5% this month. "The economy has stabilized," he says. "There are no signals that export weakness will continue." (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Taiwan's economic growth likely slowed in 3Q, as the island's exports failed to recover while private demand remained sluggish, economists say. Taiwan's GDP likely grew 2.44% on-year in 3Q, according to the median forecast of 11 economists in a Wall Street Journal poll, lower than the government's previous estimate of a 2.49% growth. Taiwan's GDP grew by 2.49% in 2Q and 1.62% in 1Q. "Weaker-than-expected exports and imports in September suggest that Taiwan's economy likely grew much slower than we had expected," says MasterLink Investment Advisory economist Anita Hsu, who revised her 3Q GDP growth forecast to 1.80% from 2.20%. Taiwan's exports in 3Q fell 0.8% on-year, compared with a 2.4% rise in both 1Q and 2Q. \uc2 Ta Chong Bank\uc2  economist Woods Chen adds, an over-5%-on-year-drop in 3Q's imports of capital equipments also indicate weak domestic demand. The government is scheduled to issue preliminary 3Q GDP data at 0030 GMT on Oct 31. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 24, 2013 01:45 ET (05:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131024e9ao000vn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
