\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -U.S. debt deal hopes lift Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 ; won hits 9-mth high\b0\par\par\uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2013\par 03:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Won rises on offshore funds bids, stop-loss dlr selling * Taiwan dlr gains on stock inflows (Adds text, updates prices) By Jongwoo Cheon SEOUL, Oct 15 (Reuters) - The South Korean won hit a near nine-month high on Tuesday as most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose on growing hopes for a deal to reopen the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  and avert a possible debt default. China's yuan hit a record high for a second consecutive session, supporting regional peers. The won rose as investors rushed to cover short positions and on sustained stock inflows, although caution over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  capped its upside, traders said. The Taiwan dollar gained on demand linked to foreign investors' stock purchases. The Thai baht also advanced, although most other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have not benefited much yet with local financial markets closed for holidays. Asian shares touched a near five-month peak as top U.S.\par \par \uc2  senators signalled they could soon reach a bipartisan deal to resume the government \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and avoid a default despite concerns over further political bickering. "It is getting priced into markets that there will be no default," said Jeong My-young, Samsung Futures research head in Seoul. "The won is likely to test this year's high and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may appreciate too." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par         \par     WON\par     The won rose as much as 0.4 percent to 1,067.0 per dollar,\par its strongest since Jan 24 on demand from offshore funds and\par stop-loss dollar selling, traders said.\par     Foreign investors extended a buying spree in Seoul's main\par stock market to a 33rd consecutive session.\par     In technical analysis terms, the won has room to head to a\par minor resistance of 1,066.3, its low on Jan 2, and eventually\par this year's high of 1,054.5, analysts said.\par     Still, apprehension grew over possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South\par Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities to stem the won's strength.\par     The won is technically seen overbought with the 14-day\par dollar/won's relative strength index below the 30 threshold,\par which indicates the dollar is oversold.\par     \par     TAIWAN DOLLAR\par     The Taiwan dollar rose on stock inflows and as strength in\par the won and the yuan provided more support.\par     Local importers bought the U.S. dollar for payments below\par 29.400, limiting the island \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's upside, traders said.\par     Investors also stayed wary of potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The Taiwan dollar is seen facing resistance at 29.300 to the\par greenback, traders added.\par     \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par   Change on the day at 0500 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen        98.46          98.59       +0.13\par  *Sing dlr        1.2423         1.2438       +0.12\par   Taiwan dlr      29.360         29.495       +0.46\par   Korean won     1067.55        1071.50       +0.37\par   Baht             31.26          31.32       +0.19\par  *P