\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2  Won Slips on Possible  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2013\par 02:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0011 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW could spike back up to the top of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel as the market trims its short-USD bets now FOMC euphoria has dissipated. Talk of \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Tuesday to prevent excessive won strength near 1,075 may also add to anxiety to buy back USD/KRW shorts around the current 8-month low. The Kospi stumbles from the open--falling 0.7% on the back of declining stocks in the U.S. on speculation Fed tapering will still spook markets in the near term. USD/KRW is now 1,076.2 from Monday's close of 1,073.8. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2348 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Shares of auto makers may soften at the market open as the dollar trends lower (now at Y98.75). Premarket bid/ask levels for \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) are at Y6,400/Y6,410 vs. Friday's closet at Y6,480 and \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2 's(7267.TO) are at Y3,840/Y3,845 vs. Friday's close at Y3,880. "Uncertainties surrounding U.S. fiscal issues and the Federal Reserve's next chairman will heighten risk-off sentiment, leading to a weaker dollar, which will weigh on Japanese exporters," says Mitsushige Akino, fund manager at Ichiyoshi Asset Management. U.S. could begin defaulting on its obligations between the end of October and the middle of November if Congress fails to raise the $16.7 trillion federal debt limit. (yumi.otagaki@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2336 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Nymex crude is likely to trade with a bearish bias near-term after hitting a six-week low of $103.12/bbl Monday, \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is negative-biased as the MACD and stochastic indicators are bearish, while the five-day moving average is below the 15-day moving average and declining. A drop below the $103.12 support would expose the downside to $102.22 (Aug. 8 reaction low), then to $102.13 (July 8 low), $101.78 (100-day moving average), $100.71 (July 5 low) and the psychological $100 line. But a rise above $105.12 (Monday's high) would temper the negative near-term view, exposing the upside to $106.46 (Friday's high), then to $108.99 (Thursday's reaction high), $109.16 (Sept. 12 reaction high), $110.46 (Sept. 9 high) and $110.70 (Sept. 6 reaction high). November crude is down 21 cents at $103.38/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2336 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Base metals could remain rangebound as conflicting views on the future of the Federal Reserve's monthly bond-buying program are likely to keep investors cautious. The Fed officials are "just going back and forth," says Charles Nedoss, senior market strategist with brokerage Kingsview Financial. Analysts say the market sentiment will be dominated by comments about the future of the stimulus program with a number of Fed officials scheduled to speak this week. The stimulus program, aimed at boosting economic growth, had underpinned base metal prices. However, upbeat Chinese manufacturing data, which reached a six-month high, is likely to provide a floor to prices. LME three-month aluminum closed 0.9% higher Monday at $1,817/ton, while copper finished 0.4% down at $7,245/ton. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China-related shares' reaction to improved China's manufacturing data may be limited, a fund manager says. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  September Preliminary China PMI rose to a six-month high of 51.2 compared with an August final of PMI 50.1. "As China's recent economic indicators have been signalling the country's economy is improving, the data itself is not a surprise," says Mitsushige Akino, fund manager at Ichiyoshi Asset Management. "The data may not offer a fresh trading cue as the market has already factored in that China's economic growth is gaining momentum," he adds. Premarket bid/ask levels for Komatsu (6301.TO) are at Y2,530/Y2,531 versus Friday's close at Y2,572 and \uc2 Hitachi Construction Machinery\uc2 's (6305.TO) are at Y2,339/Y2,340 versus Friday's close at Y2,343. Dollar trends lower below Y99 (now at Y98.80).\par \par \uc2 (yumi.otagaki@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2329 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Nikkei is likely to trade within a 200-point range Tuesday following consecutive Wall Street falls and a weaker dollar (USD/JPY now 98.78), say traders. Overnight U.S. stocks were hurt by Federal Reserve officials' suggestions that the Fed could still begin scaling back its stimulus later this year. Tokyo markets were inactive on Monday due to holiday. "Support for equities generally remains strong, but the market still feels short-term overheated, and the dollar's slide will only add another profit-taking incentive," says SMBC Nikko Securities general manager of equities Hiroichi Nishi, noting that the Nikkei remains up 6.4% since Sep. 6. Weekend German federal election results, in which Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2 's conservative party secured a strong victory, came in largely as expected, while the preliminary HSBC China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index rose to a six-month high of 51.2 in September. Nishi puts the Nikkei's expected range at 14,650 to 14,850. Nikkei 225 December futures ended yesterday's Chicago trading down 85 points at 14,585 vs Friday's close in Osaka at 14,680. In the cash market, the Nikkei closed down 0.2% at 14,742.42 Friday. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY may trade in a 98.60-99.80 range, says Kengo Suzuki, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2 . The pair fell Monday, after the Fed's Dudley said the U.S. economy has not rebounded enough to warrant a rolling back of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's easy-money policies. "Focus this week will be on comments from more Fed members," Mr. Suzuki says, tipping 98.65 as strong technical support for the pair. "The pair is weighed down by weak share prices and last week's FOMC," he says. The EUR/USD is tipped in a 1.3450-1.3520 range. The USD/JPY is at 98.77 and the EUR/USD is at 1.3493. (alexander.martin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:14 EDT - BMO Capital Markets says inflation is subdued across a broad range of components in Canada's consumer price index. In August, 72% of the 18 major categories in the CPI recorded inflation rates below the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's 2% target. "In other words...tame CPI readings look due to broad-based influences ...not rogue price declines in one particular category," BMO says. A similar dynamic prevails at the regional level, with 8 of 10 provinces recording sub-2% inflation in August, firm says. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 17:03 EDT - While the dollar will likely drift sideways in the upcoming session after last week's wild ride, BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Mike Jones\uc2  says there's risk of further weakness. Jones points to New York Fed President Dudley's remarks about iffy momentum in the US economy, which suggests improvements in the labor market might not continue. That might compel the Fed to wait until December before deciding on tapering, the official said. Investors will be watching for US housing data, German IFO and more comments from Fed officials. EUR/USD at 1.3493 early in New Zealand; USD/JPY at 98.83. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:32 EDT - RBC says the negative correlation between stocks and the DXY US dollar index recently reached its lowest level in five years. Stocks and USD began to trend upward in unison in January, it notes. "'Risk on' via equities no longer implies a weaker USD in the current environment (and vice versa)," RBC says. That also suggests an erosion in the importance of the dollar's safe-haven status as economic conditions stabilize on a global basis, firm adds. Instead, QE expectations have been the key dollar driver this year: upbeat economic data increased bets the Fed would reduce stimulus sooner, strengthening the dollar. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 15:13 EDT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  explores how monetary policy might evolve if \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  takes the helm, using an "optimal control" model she discussed in several speeches last year. That model, vs a traditional Taylor-type rule, suggests the policy rate that best gets the economy to full employment while maintaining price stability would be held down longer. It risks inflation drifting above target in a few years to get the jobless rate down further. Based on this, Goldman forecasts the first hike for early 2016, compared to mid-2015 as priced into markets. In addition to this model, Goldman warns "the unemployment rate understates the labor market slack," which argues monetary policy should stay accommodative for longer. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 13:03 EDT - US Treasurys are on track to hand owners their first positive monthly return since April. With the Fed sticking around buying bonds at full throttle, traders says buyers have taken the upper hand after a summer of sellers dominating. Investors may also find renewed safe-haven appeal in Treasurys as the US budget debate heats up. The market has returned 0.23% so far this month on the \uc2 Barclays\uc2  index, bringing year-to-date returns to -2.5%. RBS sees the next key selling level at 2.45% on 10-year notes. Ten-years recently up 8/32 to yield 2.706%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj) [ 24-09-13 0011GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130924e99o00005\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
