\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -China factory survey lifts won, Taiwan dlr; SE Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  falls\b0\par\par\uc2 855 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2013\par 04:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Won hits 8-mth high on stock inflows\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Taiwan dlr at 4-month peak on foreign demand, exporters\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  limits won, Taiwan dlr upside -traders\par     * Rupiah down on corp dlr demand; c.bank sells dlrs\par  (Adds details, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, Sept 23 (Reuters) - The South Korean won and the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Taiwan dollar hit multi-month highs on Monday, helped by more signs of China's economic turnaround, while most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid on renewed worries that the Federal Reserve will cut its monetary stimulus. Growth in China's factory sector accelerated to a six-month high in September, a preliminary survey showed, as stronger domestic and foreign demand added to recent signs of a tentative turnaround in the world's second-largest economy. The won touched a near eight-month peak on sustained stock inflows. The Taiwan dollar advanced to its strongest in more than four months on demand from foreign financial institutions and exporters. The Northeast Asian units found further support from catch-up plays as their financial markets were closed late last week when the Fed surprised global investors by standing pat, which boosted regional peers. In contrast, most Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell on Monday after a top Fed official suggested there is a chance of tapering next month, although those units pared some losses after the encouraging China survey. "It reflects a theme of mild outperformance in new industrialised economies' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not just catch-up plays even as we see cautious environment following the Fed non-taper move last week," said Saktiandi Supaat, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  research for Maybank in Singapore, referring to Singapore, South Korea and Taiwan. "They will keep outperforming on better fundamentals, correlation and more direct links with developed markets' recovery as long as the uncertainty of a taper or an eventual taper does not derail the U.S. or developed market recovery," he added. The won and the Taiwan dollar have weathered concerns over the Fed's policy shift better than Southeast Asian peers as their high-tech exports have benefited from a global recovery.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     \par     WON\par     The won advanced 0.8 percent to 1,075.0 per dollar as\par foreign investors extended a buying spree to a 19th session in\par Seoul's main stock exchange.\par     Still, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities were\par spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem its upside, traders said.\par     Such official dollar purchases prevented exporters from\par chasing the won for month-end settlements, they added.\par     "Everybody is cautious over the authorities here. China PMI\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 added support, but the won is likely to stay around the current levels," a foreign bank trader in Seoul, referring to the flash \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Purchasing Managers' Index.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     China is South Korea's top export market.\par     \par     TAIWAN DOLLAR\par     The Taiwan dollar rose as much as 0.8 percent to 29.505 to\par the U.S. dollar, its strongest since May 10, on inflows from\par foreign financial institutions and exporters' demand.\par     But its upside was limited in thin trade as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par was spotted buying small amounts of U.S. dollars around 29.520,\par traders said.\par     Local importers also purchased the greenback for payments,\par while some foreign institutions joined them, according to\par traders.\par         \par     RUPIAH\par     The rupiah lost ground on increasing dollar demand from\par local companies for month-end payments.\par     The forwards market pointed to further depreciation with\par one-month non-deliverable forwards to the dollar \par weakening to 11,310.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted providing dollar liquidity\par around the session low of 11,470 per dollar, traders said.\par     "BI wants to maintain that level first," said a\par Jakarta-based trader, referring to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     "But the rupiah will weaken more this week as dollar demand\par is intensifying," he added.\par     \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \par   Change on the day at 0430 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen        99.12          99.31       +0.19\par   Sing dlr        1.2490         1.2523       +0.26\par   Taiwan dlr      29.570         29.752       +0.62\par   Korean won     1075.80        1084.10       +0.77\par   Baht             31.12          30.95       -0.56\par   Peso             43.16          43.06       -0.24\par   Rupiah        11470.00       11340.00       -1.13\par   Rupee            62.37          62.23       -0.22\par   Ringgit         3.1710         3.1645       -0.20\par   Yuan            6.1180         6.1212       +0.05\par  \par   Change so far in 2013\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen        99