\par\par \b \uc2 USD/KRW Pops up on USD/JPY Strength; 1,090 to Be Tested -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2013\par 06:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0431 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The rally in USD/JPY to a two-month high of 100.60 is pushing the USD/KRW higher, too. USD/KRW, currently near its intraday peak of 1,089.7, is likely to test resistance at 1,090. Stop-loss orders waiting above may get triggered which could send the dollar to the top of the daily Bollinger downtrend channel at 1,095. The won typically trades in tandem with the Japanese yen. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  watches the yen/won exchange rate to maintain price competitiveness for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exporters--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when necessary via USD/KRW to crimp excessive moves in the won. USD/KRW is now 1,088.8. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0430 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The NZD/USD is underperforming in Asia as investors are a "bit wary of Governor Wheeler talking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down tomorrow" at the RBNZ's monetary policy review, says Tim Kelleher, ASB's head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  institutional sales. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  is unanimously expected to keep its official cash rate on hold at 2.5%, according to a Wall Street Journal poll of 13 economists. However, "it will be interesting to see what the bank's impressions of the LVR (loan-to-value) restrictions are and what impact they think they will have," Kelleher says. Overall, the market is "a little bit defensive." The pair is at 0.8047 and Kelleher tips support at 0.8000 and resistance at 0.8100. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0404 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Federal Reserve's two-day policy meeting next week will be "back on the radar" for gold investors amid easing tensions over possible U.S. military action against Syria, \uc2 OCBC Bank\uc2  says in a report. Gold prices have been underpinned in recent days as escalating tensions over Syria triggered demand for the precious metal, considered a safe-haven asset during times of geopolitical and economic turmoil. "Gold demand as a safe-haven asset dulled while concerns over QE [quantitative easing] tapering sent gold back to its $1,360/oz handle at closing yesterday," OCBC says. Gold prices have been underpinned in the last couple of years as increased liquidity injection by the Fed increased demand for the metal, which is also considered to be a tool to fight inflation. Spot gold is at $1,364.40/oz, up $1.10 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/MYR slips to 3.2740 vs 3.2790 late Tuesday in Asia as the easing of tensions over Syria supports risk sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The bias remains on the downside as indicated by moving average convergence divergence forest, though the pair could consolidate ahead of the (July) industrial production data out later today," says Maybank in a note. The house tips the pair to trade between 3.2576 and 3.2838. The factory output data is due at 0401 GMT. The median forecast in \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  survey of 11 economists is for a 4.8% on-year rise. (abhrajit.gangopadhyay@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/PHP is lower in early trade, tracking the dollar's broad weakness against most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid easing concerns over Syria. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair is at 43.77 vs 43.83 late Tuesday in Manila. "The market is watching for any possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially if some banks try to push the dollar lower," says a local bank trader. Volume is moderate. Traders expect the USD/PHP to stay in a 43.50-44.00 range in the near term. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0254 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/IDR NDF market is showing signs of a relief rally coming for the rupiah. The premium for the 1-month USD/IDR contract, which traded as high as IDR350 above the spot contract price last week, is now down to around IDR130. This premium typically ranges between IDR20 and IDR40 in normal market conditions, but has risen sharply since August due to the emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  selloff. Though the country's current account deficit and fiscal woes still weigh on the rupiah, the broader picture of reduced USD strength -- due to easing Syria tensions and the possibility of a more gradual Fed tapering of monetary stimulus -- is helping to improve investors' risk appetite. USD/IDR is now at 11,300. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/PHP gapped lower at Wednesday's opening, implying more peso strength. But those peso gains were quickly erased, due to buying on dips, which sent USD/PHP to 43.80 from 43.65. The peso has hit a peak of 1.9% appreciation since Friday -- an outstanding gain in just three days -- though the rebound in peso strength was mainly due to the USD capitulation, which is why further downside may not be as forthcoming. The U.S. Federal Reserve may still decide to taper quantitative easing at the FOMC meeting this month, in spite of lukewarm U.S. job creation. USD/PHP is now 43.77. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/TWD rebounds on buying from foreign investors, two local traders say. The pair is at 29.750--higher than the 29.660 before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday which lifted it to 29.760 at the close of onshore trading, traders say. "Local stocks are falling amid concerns over domestic political rows with foreign investors likely turning net sellers of local stocks after being net buyers for the past 10 sessions," a trader says tipping the pair between 29.700-29.800 for the session. The Taiex is 0.6% lower. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/THB seems unwilling to trade much lower despite the broadly weaker USD. The pair barely tested the 32.00 psychological barrier Tuesday, and instead ended the day higher - a contrarian move in the region. USD/THB may be stuck in a 32.00-32.20 range for now. The baht may be bogged down by the rubber farmers' protests - though the government has already agreed to double its initial subsidy offer. But some analysts are also worried about slowing export growth for the nation, which would crimp GDP growth over the next few quarters. Thailand entered a technical recession last quarter, chalking up two straight quarters of negative GDP. USD/THB is now 32.09. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 China International Capital Corporation\uc2  has raised its forecast for China's gross domestic product growth for the third quarter and all of 2013 with the nation's industrial sector showing some signs of strength. The house expects China's GDP to expand 7.8% on-year in the third quarter, up from an earlier forecast of a 7.6% growth, and it tips full year growth at 7.6% vs a previous estimate of 7.5%. "August saw a continued recovery of economic growth, with domestic and external demand both picking up," writes CICC chief economist Peng Wensheng in a note. Peng adds that state-owned enterprises are the major driver of expanded production and investment. Industrial output in August climbed 10.4% on year, up from 9.7% in July, chalking up its highest growth rate since March 2012. (liyan.qi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] With the constituent \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the SGD NEER (nominal effective exchange rate) today reacting to the softer dollar, the NEER boundaries for the USD-SGD is also shading lower, says OCBC, with the extremities of the fluctuation band currently estimated at 1.2540 and 1.2955 respectively. "In the interim, USD-SGD may be expected to ply a 1.2630-1.2730 band intra-day," the bank says. Meanwhile, UOB expects the USD/SGD today to trade in a narrower 1.2641-1.2705 range, between 0.5% below its estimated midpoint of the NEER and the midpoint. Spot USD/SGD was last quoted at 1.2683. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] DBS expects \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  to hold its benchmark rate steady at 2.50% at Thursday's meeting (announcement due 0100 GMT), cites little reason to hike rates as inflation remains low (1.3% Y/Y in August) and, unlike some other emerging markets, the BOK doesn't face pressure to contain capital outflows and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation. DBS also says cutting rates is unlikely as the BOK holds the view that the Korean economy will continue to recover in second half 2013 and the negative output gap will narrow further. "With the U.S. and Japan growing steadily, euro zone exiting recession and China recently showing some signs of bottoming-out, policymakers in Korea should have confidence to maintain their forecast of a recovery," the bank adds. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) [ 11-09-13 0431GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130911e99b00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
