\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: USD/JPY Takes RBA, China Data In Stride - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2013\par 04:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The USD/JPY takes the RBA statement and China data largely in stride, says a senior dealer at a Japanese bank. The RBA cutting its economic growth forecast was negative for the AUD, he says. But various China data, largely in line with expectations, soothed any concerns over a sharp slowdown for Asia's largest economy, which helps support the AUD, he says. The USD/JPY is likely to trade in a 96.00-97.00 range for the global day, he says. The pair is at 96.56.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Factory gate prices in China show some signs of stabilizing, according to Grace Ng, an economist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 . The producer price index, which tracks wholesale prices, fell 2.3% on-year in July compared to 2.7% in June. "On the global stage there's been a modest upturn in commodity prices recently, including oil," said Ms. Ng. "We've also seen some indications that demand for upstream products has been stabilizing. Inventory levels have been coming down." Analysts have long worried that falling producer prices are a bitter pill for companies in low-margin businesses like steel, aluminium and cement - although at least part of the deflation has come from lower input prices. The PPI has been in falling since March 2012. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 The USD/JPY takes the RBA statement and China data largely in stride, says a senior dealer at a Japanese bank. The RBA cutting its economic growth forecast was negative for the AUD, he says. But various China data, largely in line with expectations, soothed any concerns over a sharp slowdown for Asia's largest economy, which helps support the AUD, he says. The USD/JPY is likely to trade in a 96.00-97.00 range for the global day, he says. The pair is at 96.56.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The USD/TWD is flat with foreign investors' buying offsetting export settlements, says a local trader. The pair is at 29.940, little changed from the 29.930 level it was at before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, which lifted it to 29.960 at the close of the onshore trading session, traders say. "Foreign investors' buying intensifies around NT$29.910 as their selling in local stocks continues," a trader says, adding that the market will likely continue seeking cues from foreign fund movements in local stocks, with the Taiex last down 0.1%. He tips the pair in a 29.900-29.950 band for the session. Foreign investors' net selling was at NT$11.82 billion Thursday--the largest single-day net selling in more than six weeks. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The USD/TWD is flat with foreign investors' buying offsetting export settlements, says a local trader. The pair is at 29.940, little changed from the 29.930 level it was at before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, which lifted it to 29.960 at the close of the onshore trading session, traders say. "Foreign investors' buying intensifies around NT$29.910 as their selling in local stocks continues," a trader says, adding that the market will likely continue seeking cues from foreign fund movements in local stocks, with the Taiex last down 0.1%. He tips the pair in a 29.900-29.950 band for the session. Foreign investors' net selling was at NT$11.82 billion Thursday--the largest single-day net selling in more than six weeks. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China's consumer inflation held steady at 2.7% on-year in July, the same as the month before and just behind the 2.8% rise forecast by economists. But inflation still looks unthreatening and remains well inside the government's target of keeping yearly price increases to 3.5% or less. The producer price index, which tracks wholesale prices, remained in deflationary territory. But price declines eased a little, with the PPI down 2.3% on-year, compared with June's 2.7% decline. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China's consumer inflation held steady at 2.7% on-year in July, the same as the month before and just behind the 2.8% rise forecast by economists. But inflation still looks unthreatening and remains well inside the government's target of keeping yearly price increases to 3.5% or less. The producer price index, which tracks wholesale prices, remained in deflationary territory. But price declines eased a little, with the PPI down 2.3% on-year, compared with June's 2.7% decline. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China's inflation likely ticked up slightly in July as food prices staged a rebound, economists say. The consumer price index likely rose 2.8% on-year, according to the median forecast of 14 economists surveyed by \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . That is a rise from 2.7% in June, though it remains well within the government's target of keeping price rises below 3.5%. Food prices are adding a dose of inflationary pressure, with the Agricultural Wholesale Price Index up 6% on year in July. Meanwhile the producer price index, tracking wholesale prices, likely saw an easing of deflationary pressure. The poll predicts a 2.2% on-year decline in July, compared with a 2.7% fall in June. The National Bureau of Statistics is due to release the data at 0130 GMT. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China's inflation likely ticked up slightly in July as food prices staged a rebound, economists say. Th