the 2.8% rise forecast by economists. But inflation still looks unthreatening and remains well inside the government's target of keeping yearly price increases to 3.5% or less. The producer price index, which tracks wholesale prices, remained in deflationary territory. But price declines eased a little, with the PPI down 2.3% on-year, compared with June's 2.7% decline. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones] China's inflation likely ticked up slightly in July as food prices staged a rebound, economists say. The consumer price index likely rose 2.8% on-year, according to the median forecast of 14 economists surveyed by Dow Jones. That is a rise from 2.7% in June, though it remains well within the government's target of keeping price rises below 3.5%. Food prices are adding a dose of inflationary pressure, with the Agricultural Wholesale Price Index up 6% on year in July. Meanwhile the producer price index, tracking wholesale prices, likely saw an easing of deflationary pressure. The poll predicts a 2.2% on-year decline in July, compared with a 2.7% fall in June. The National Bureau of Statistics is due to release the data at 0130 GMT. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones] China's inflation likely ticked up slightly in July as food prices staged a rebound, economists say. The consumer price index likely rose 2.8% on-year, according to the median forecast of 14 economists surveyed by Dow Jones. That is a rise from 2.7% in June, though it remains well within the government's target of keeping price rises below 3.5%. Food prices are adding a dose of inflationary pressure, with the Agricultural Wholesale Price Index up 6% on year in July. Meanwhile the producer price index, tracking wholesale prices, likely saw an easing of deflationary pressure. The poll predicts a 2.2% on-year decline in July, compared with a 2.7% fall in June. The National Bureau of Statistics is due to release the data at 0130 GMT. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW rebounds to 1,111.30 from an earlier low of 1,108.00 but remains down from 1,113.00 late Thursday. The pair is downbeat due to global weakness in the greenback and the foreign selling of South Korean stocks, but foreign buying of South Korean bonds helps temper the fall, Hyundai Futures Co., Ltd. analyst Lee Dae-ho. Lee sees the pair trading close to the 1,110 level but says that support will break later this month; "Depending on events in the U.S., I see the 1,100 level at risk." (jeyup.kwaak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW rebounds to 1,111.30 from an earlier low of 1,108.00 but remains down from 1,113.00 late Thursday. The pair is downbeat due to global weakness in the greenback and the foreign selling of South Korean stocks, but foreign buying of South Korean bonds helps temper the fall, Hyundai Futures Co., Ltd. analyst Lee Dae-ho. Lee sees the pair trading close to the 1,110 level but says that support will break later this month; "Depending on events in the U.S., I see the 1,100 level at risk." (jeyup.kwaak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones] Positive signals from the eurozone and China will push the greenback down further on Friday, with the USD/KRW expected to slide below 1,110 from 1,113.00 late Thursday in Seoul, says Son Eun-jeong, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Woori Futures, tipping support at 1,108. Recent movements haven't been large due to peak summer vacation season and holidays in Singapore, Malaysia, Indonesia and India, says Son. Once below the 1,110 level, downward momentum will weaken as traders expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (jeyup.kwaak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones] Positive signals from the eurozone and China will push the greenback down further on Friday, with the USD/KRW expected to slide below 1,110 from 1,113.00 late Thursday in Seoul, says Son Eun-jeong, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Woori Futures, tipping support at 1,108. Recent movements haven't been large due to peak summer vacation season and holidays in Singapore, Malaysia, Indonesia and India, says Son. Once below the 1,110 level, downward momentum will weaken as traders expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (jeyup.kwaak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude is likely to consolidate for now after hitting a one-month low of $102.22 Thursday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is still negative-biased as the MACD and slow stochastic indicators are bearish. Resistance is at $104.96 (Thursday's high); a breach would expose the upside to $105.77 (Wednesday's high), then to $107.27 (Tuesday's high), $107.69 (Monday's high), $108.82 (Aug. 2 reaction high) and $109.32 (near-17-month high hit July 19). But a drop below the $102.22 support would target $102.13 (July 8 low), then $100.71 (July 5 low), the 55-day moving average (now at $100.66) and the psychological $100 line. September crude is up 36 cents at $103.76/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may rise as the unwinding of short positions is likely to continue on the back of expected gains in stocks, with focus on Chinese data due later, says a senior dealer at a major European bank in Tokyo. "JPY crosses are receiving bids," he says; "The downside appears firm." He says the key is whether the pair ends above 97.30 for the day, which signals the JPY's weakening trend will continue. Nikkei September futures end at 13,650 in Chicago Thursday vs 13,620 in Osaka. The USD/JPY is at 96.74. As for EUR/JPY, gains above 129.50 signal more room for upside, he says. The cross is at 129.41. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may rise as the unwinding of short positions is likely to continue on the back of expected gains in stocks, with focu