\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Weak China PMI trims Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  gains; rupiah falls again\b0\par\par\uc2 847 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 2013\par 04:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Indian c.bank takes new steps to support rupee * Won at 7-wk high on offshore funds, exporters * Rupiah at 4-yr low; indicative prices match dealt levels (Adds text, updates prices) By Jongwoo Cheon SINGAPORE, July 24 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened on Wednesday, led by the Indian rupee and the South Korean won, but gains were pared as China manufacturing activity increased worries about a slowdown in the world's No.2 economy. The day's big loser was Indonesia's rupiah, which touched its weakest in more than four years as indicative prices matched market levels, as on Tuesday. Traders said month-end corporate dollar-demand fueled the fall. The rupee gained after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took new steps on Tuesday to support the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , signalling it will stay the course with its defence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the risks to economic growth. The won hit a near seven-week high on demand from offshore funds and exporters.\par \par \uc2  But those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and regional peers gave up initial gains as the latest reading on China's manufacturing activity showed activity slowed to an 11-month low in July as new orders faltered and the job market darkened. Bad news from China combined with no signals of some stimulus measures "may worsen sentiment", said Saktiandi Supaat, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  research for Maybank in Singapore. Still, the slowdown in China's economy has already been priced into emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to some degree, and any further downside will be limited, Supaat said. Some traders agreed with him, saying regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to find support amid expectations that the Federal Reserve may not taper its monetary stimulus soon, given recent weak U.S. data. "China data has been soft for the past three months. They have already talked about painful reforms," said a senior trader at a Malaysian bank in Kuala Lumpur, adding he would buy the ringgit on dips for a target of 3.1500 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par         \par     WON\par     At its strongest point, the won was up 0.4 percent to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1,112.8 per dollar, its highest level June 7, as offshore model funds bought it on overnight weakness in the greenback, traders said. South Korean exporters including shipbuilders joined the bids, traders added. Technically, the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may head to the previous high of 1,107.6 on June 7 once if ends the session stronger than 1,113.8, the 61.8 percent Fibonacci retracement of its depreciation between May and June, analysts said. Some traders, however, hesitated to chase the won on growing caution over potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem its appreciation, especially when China, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's top export market, is losing growth momentum. "Who dares to push it further from here in the summer doldrums?" said a foreign bank trader in Seoul. "Dollar-short plays will be dead if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities absorb even a small amount of dollars," the trader added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     \par     RUPIAH\par     The rupiah fell as much as 0.8 percent to 10,265 per dollar,\par the weakest since June 2009 on sustained dollar demand from\par local companies.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was spotted selling dollar at the session\par low to limit the rupiah's downside, while it continued to allow\par indicative prices to reflect traded prices, traders said.\par     On Tuesday, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  let indicative rupiah prices it\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 puts on screens match different levels used in Jakarta's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market dealings to lure more banks to provide more dollar liquidity. "Overall, the market is still bullish on dollar/rupiah," said a Jakarta-based trader. "But BI is there to maintain certain ranges. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at current levels is better. BI's step can reduce volatility and the spread between bid and offer," the trader added. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0510 GMT\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen        99.70          99.44       -0.26\par   Sing dlr        1.2649         1.2638       -0.09\par   Taiwan dlr      29.900         29.920       +0.07\par   Korean won     1113.80        1117.00       +0.29\par   Baht             30.90          30.95       +0.16\par   Peso             43.24          43.23       -0.01\par   Rupiah        10250.00       10180.00       -0.68\par   Rupee            59.45          59.76       +0.52\par   Ringgit         3.1780         3.1785       +0.02\par   Yuan            6.1370         6.1374       +0.01\par  \par   Change so far in 2013\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen        99.70          86.79      -12.95\par   Sing dlr        1.2649         1.2219       -3.40\par   Taiwan dlr      29.900        