\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: South Korea Revises Up Growth Forecast\b0\par\par\uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2013\par 09:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- South Korea raises 2013 growth outlook, cuts inflation forecast\par \par \uc2 --The government says extra budget spending, other stimulus will help boost growth this year\par \par \uc2 -- Korea runs record-high current-account surplus in May\par \par \uc2 (Combines two stories, context and changes thoughout)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Kwanwoo Jun and In-Soo Nam \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL--South Korea on Thursday revised up its growth forecast for this year and reported a record-high monthly current account surplus, reflecting the resilience and the impact of stimulus on Asia's fourth-largest economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Robust exports helped balloon the current-account surplus to $8.64 billion in May, the biggest monthly amount on record and more than double the previous month's, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The current account data are the broadest measure of South Korea's trade with the rest of the world.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said South Korea's economy is likely to grow 2.7% this year, rather than 2.3% projected in March, as it expects stimulus efforts to "start taking effect on a full scale" later in the year. It forecasts 2014 gross domestic product growth of 4.0%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The economy will gradually pick up on improving external conditions and stimulus policy effects are beginning in earnest," the finance ministry said in its latest economic outlook. It highlighted volatile foreign capital flows as a potential risk to growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the government's rosy outlook, some analysts said South Korea faces headwinds as the Chinese economy is slowing and the weakening yen is hitting South Korean exports by making Japanese products relatively cheaper.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More recently, concerns the U.S. could withdraw its economic stimulus and signs that China's economy is slowing have roiled the South Korean markets. Investors fear the economy could slow this year if money is attracted to the U.S. from emerging markets, while a slowdown in China would be bad for Korean exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But authorities have not been sitting on their hands and various measures have been taken to stimulate South Korea's slowing economy. It grew 2% last year, the slowest pace in three years, after 3.6% growth in 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Parliament in May approved extra government spending of 17.3 trillion won ($14.97 billion), as well as measures aimed at creating jobs and supporting the property market and small companies. Acting in tandem, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lowered its policy interest rate by a quarter percentage point to 2.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Quarterly economic growth has been below 1% for two years, but signs of acceleration were seen in the first quarter of this year, and the economy should benefit from a likely increase in export demand in the second half of 2013, the ministry said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Hyun Oh-seok told a press conference the government would let economic fundamentals determine how the won trades in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets but reaffirmed it would take action to ease volatility. Traders suspect that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's financial authorities have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in recent weeks, to curb unusual spikes and excessive falls in the won.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ministry said it now expects 300,000 jobs to be created this year, rather than the 250,000 it projected in March.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It sees the consumer price index rising 1.7% this year, rather than 2.3% earlier forecast, which would be far below the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target range of 2.5% to 3.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Its current account surplus forecast is now $38 billion, from its earlier projection of $29 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current-account balance remained in the black for the 16th consecutive month for South Korea, which last posted a deficit in January 2012, according the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . [ 06-27-13 0343ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020130627e96r0000u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
