\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Fed fears, China worries hit Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 ; ringgit at 3-yr low\b0\par\par\uc2 1,004 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2013\par 09:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Corrects typo in first paragraph, adds dropped word in third paragraph)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Ringgit, baht, Philippine peso down on weak stocks, bonds\par     * Won at 1-yr low on foreign stock selling, offshore funds\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 South Korea intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in spot won not seen - traders\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, June 24 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended declines on Monday, with the Malaysian ringgit at a near three-year low, as rising \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields fueled fears of more capital outflows as the Federal Reserve prepares to scale back stimulus measures.     Worries about China's economic and stability also dragged regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, traders said.     The ringgit, the Thai baht and the Philippine peso came under pressure from weaker bonds in those countries, while the South Korean won hit a one-year low as foreign investors kept selling local stocks.     "The macro outlook this week for Asia and its relevance for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to take a back seat to the direction of U.S. interest rates and the general outflows from Asian equity and fixed income markets," Scotiabank said in a client note.     The 10-Year Treasury note yield rose to 2.6135 percent, the highest since August 2011, late on Monday in Asia.     Emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  suffered a heavy sell-off last week, with the Indian rupee hitting a fresh record low. The ringgit and the Philippine peso saw their worst weekly slides in about three years.     Fed Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  on June 19 signalled the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would taper its bond-buying programme starting later this year and end the quantitative easing by the middle of 2014.     That spurred investors to flee from emerging Asian assets, which they had accumulated in past years using easy money printed by major central banks including the Fed.     But investors were wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by regional central banks to stem their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' slide.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     \par     RINGGIT\par     The ringgit lost as much as 0.5 percent to 3.2190 per\par dollar, the weakest since July 2010, as custodian banks and\par offshore funds sold the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders said.\par     Five-year government bond yields rose to 3.572\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 percent, the highest since May 2011, and three-year yields\par  advanced to 3.398 percent. The 10-year yields\par  stayed at 3.633 percent, the highest since April\par 2012.\par     But some traders said the ringgit's recent weakness is\par excessive. It lost 2.7 percent against the dollar last week, the\par largest weekly decline since May 2010, according to Thomson\par Reuters data.\par     "I may want to take profits from dollar around 3.218 and\par 3.22. I am waiting for signs of exhaustion of dollar/Asia's\par upside," said a senior Malaysian bank trader in Kuala Lumpur.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     But the trader said he would buy the dollar around 3.2000.\par     \par     WON\par     The won hit a fresh one-year low of 1,161.5, the weakest\par since late June 2012, as foreign investors kept selling Seoul\par shares and on dollar demand from offshore funds.\par     Foreigners were net sellers in the main stock market \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 for a 12th straight session. They have dumped a combined net 5.4\par trillion won ($4.7 billion) in shares during the period,\par according to the Korea Exchange data.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities were barely spotted buying the\par won, defying caution over such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and prompting\par traders to cover short positions in the dollar, traders said.\par     "The won is not free from weakness in regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par despite potential efforts by the authorities to stabilise the\par market," said a senior South Korean bank trader in Seoul.\par     "The won is likely to gradually decline with dollar/won's\par lows elevating," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     \par     PHILIPPINE PESO\par     The peso slid as Manila stocks lost 3.5 percent.\par     The 10-year government bond yields and the\par 5-year yields continued to rise.\par     But investors stayed cautious as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was\par expected to step in the market to limit its downside. The\par authority was spotted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week, traders said.\par     \par     BAHT\par     The baht fell in thin trade with softer local stocks and\par bonds, traders said.\par     The 10-year Thai government bond yields rose to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4.06 percent, the highest since July 2011. The 5-year yields\par  advanced to 3.47 percent, also the highest since\par early June last year.\par     Bangkok shares lost 0.7 percent.\par     Investors are keeping an eye on possible baht bids linked to\par Japanese Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group's (MUFG) \par potential acquisition of a majority stake in Thailand's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of\par Ayudhya Pcl\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     MUFG is in advanced talks to buy the stake in a deal worth\par about $4.1 billion, sources familiar with the matter said on\par Saturday. \par     \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par   Change on the day at 0700 GMT\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen        98.44          97.82       -0.63\par   Sing dlr        1.2800         1.2764       -0.28\par   Taiwan dlr      30.147         30.280       +0.44\par   Korean won     1161.25        1154.70       -0.56\par   Baht             31.10          31.01       -0.29\par   Peso             43.91          43.72       -0.43\par   Rupiah         9930.00        9920.00       -0.10\par   Rupee            59.80          59.27       -0.89\par   Ringgit         3.2170         3.2015       -0.48\par   Yuan            6.1395         6.1342       -0.09\par  \par   Change so far in 2013\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen        98.44          86.79      -11.83\par   Sing dlr        1.2800         1.2219       -4.54\par   Taiwan dlr      30.147         29.136       -3.35\par   Korean won     1161.25        1070.60       -7.81\par   Baht             31.10          30.61       -1.58\par   Peso             43.91          41.05       -6.51\par   Rupiah         9930.00        9630.00       -3.02\par   Rupee            59.80          54.99       -8.04\par   Ringgit         3.2170         3.0580       -4.94\par   Yuan            6.1395         6.2303       +1.48\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  ($1 = 1152.2500 Korean won)\par \par  (Additional reporting by Lee Kyoung-ho in SEOUL; Editing by Kim\par Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (UPDATE 1, CORRECTED) | ABX | ABN | D | E | RBN | IF | J | M | PHD | SNI | SI | Z | SNS | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130627e96o000az\par }\page {\pa