\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won leads Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  gains after selloff; ringgit hit\b0\par\par\uc2 996 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2013\par 02:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Won up 1 pct on exporters, offshore funds\par \par \uc2  * Ringgit sees worst day in 8 mths on catch-up selling\par \par \uc2  * Baht up on stock inflows, exporters\par \par \uc2  * Corporate demand lifts Philippine peso \par \par \uc2 (Adds text, updates prices)\par \par \uc2  By Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 29 (Reuters) - The South Korean won had its best day in 13 months, leading gains that most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  produced on Tuesday as investors saw their recent weakness as excessive given the region's solid economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2  The won ended the local trade up 1.0 percent against the dollar, its largest daily percentage gain since Dec. 21, 2011, according to Thomson Reuters data.\par \par \uc2  The Thai baht strengthened on stock inflows and exporters' demand for settlements. The Philippine peso\par \par \uc2 rose as corporate demand caused investors to cover short positions.\par \par \uc2  But the Malaysian ringgit suffered its largest daily fall in more than eight months on catch-up selling after a holiday on Monday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also told domestic banks to use a reference rate produced by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  association for ringgit \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  contracts.\par \par \uc2  "Recent slides by emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were seen as overdone, even though they may see more downside in the short term, given \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars and possible short-covering in the euro against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Yuna Park, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond analyst at \uc2 Dongbu Securities\uc2  in Seoul.\par \par \uc2  "Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  will eventually rise in the longer term," Park added.\par \par \uc2  Most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened at the start of this year, but then weakened after investors booked profits from their 2012 gains amid concerns that the units had risen too far against a weakening yen.\par \par \uc2  The won's surge on Tuesday came one day after it suffered its largest daily fall in 16 months on selling from offshore funds and stock outflows.\par \par \uc2  WON\par \par \uc2  The South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded on exporters' demand for month-end settlements and as offshore macro funds bought it after the previous session's selloff.\par \par \uc2  Earlier, its gains were limited by dollar demand linked to foreign investors' recent stock sales with custodian banks' dollar bids, dealers said.\par \par \uc2  But sustained demand from exporters prompted stop-loss dollar selling, they added.\par \par \uc2 Skandinaviska Enskilda Banken\uc2  (SEB) offered a trade idea to buy the on dips, given South Korea's export recovery and expected bond inflows. Market positions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not crowded after recent slides, it added.\par \par \uc2  However, SEB said the risk posed by buying on dips is that a weaker yen could trigger more active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially if China's yuan fixing is not firm.\par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may become more active if the two biggest neighbours, China and Japan, have weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," it said in a note.\par \par \uc2  BAHT\par \par \uc2  The baht rose on exporters' demand and stock-related inflows.\par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  accelerated its gain after strengthening past 29.90 per dollar, dealers said.\par \par \uc2  But its upside was capped by month-end dollar bids from local importers.\par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO\par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso rebounded as corporate bids caused investors to cover short positions in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  A foreign bank dealer said the peso is expected to return to an appreciation trend again, adding: "investors are not waiting for 41.00 to sell dollar/peso anymore."\par \par \uc2  RINGGIT\par \par \uc2  The ringgit fell 1.1 percent to 3.0760 per dollar on demand for the greenback from sovereign funds, custodian banks and leveraged accounts. Investors also covered dollar-short positions a day after a holiday on Monday.\par \par \uc2  But some interbank speculators bought the ringgit on dips.\par \par \uc2  If the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintains the loss, it would be its largest daily percentage fall since May 16, 2012, according to Thomson Reuters data. Earlier, the ringgit touched 3.0810, its weakest since Oct. 10.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market players did not show immediate reaction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move on fixing, although a trader said it caused dollar-short covering.\par \par \uc2  When financial markets in Malaysia were closed on Monday, other emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were hit by worries about outflows.\par \par \uc2  RUPIAH\par \par \uc2  The rupiah's indicative prices slid 0.8 percent to 9,750 per dollar, while it traded above 9,800 on month-end corporate dollar demand, dealers said.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted providing dollar liquidity at lower levels, such as 9,770, they added.\par \par \uc2  Some dealers expected the rupiah to stabilise once importers' dollar bids waned by the end of the month, given strong determination by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Still, the rupiah is likely to stay softer as long as investors remain worried about the size of the country's current account deficit, dealers said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par \par \uc2  Change on the day at 0812 GMT\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        90.66          90.86       +0.22\par  Sing dlr        1.2363         1.2383       +0.16\par  Taiwan dlr      29.546         29.560       +0.05\par  Korean won     1082.65        1093.50       +1.00\par  Baht             29.83          29.98       +0.50\par  Peso             40.72          40.91       +0.47\par  Rupiah         9730.00        9670.00       -0.62\par  Rupee            53.66          53.91       +0.47\par  Ringgit         3.0760         3.0430       -1.07\par  Yuan            6.2241         6.2226       -0.02\par  Change so far in 2013\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        90.66          86.79       -4.27\par  Sing dlr        1.2363         1.2219       -1.16\par  Taiwan dlr      29.546         29.136       -1.39\par  Korean won     1082.65        1070.60       -1.11\par  Baht             29.83          30.61       +2.61\par  Peso             40.72          41.05       +0.81\par  Rupiah         9730.00        9630.00       -1.03\par  Rupee            53.66          54.99       +2.48\par  Ringgit         3.0760         3.0580       -0.59\par  Yuan            6.2241         6.2303       +0.10\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by IFR Markets' Catherine Tan; Editing by