\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:Seoul Issues A Warning As Slumping Yen Hits South Korea's Competitiveness\b0\par\par\uc2 878 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2013\par 14:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Michael S. Arnold, Mari Iwata and Kwanwoo Jun \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG--\f28 \uc2 \f2 South Korea's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor on Monday defended Seoul's moves to restrain the surging won, especially while the neighboring yen has tumbled.\f28 \par \par \uc2 But Seoul could get some relief as Japan itself starts to worry that the yen may have fallen too far, too fast.\par \par \uc2 The rare and explicit comments by \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2 's \uc2 Kim Choong-soo\uc2  mark one of the first direct references from an economic policymaker outside Japan to the impact of Tokyo's campaign to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen has fallen from around 79 to the dollar to nearly 90 since Japan's Parliament was dissolved in November, paving the way for the election of the Liberal Democratic Party's Shinzo Abe as prime minister. Mr. Abe has made a weaker yen a centerpiece of his push to revitalize Japan's economy, exerting fierce pressure on the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to pursue virtually unlimited monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 The sagging yen has thrown a lifeline to Japanese exporters who for the past three years have suffered from a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regarded as the ultimate "safe haven" in times of global turbulence. For South Korea, however, the yen's plunge comes just as the won is reaching near 18-month highs against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "In the event that exchange rate volatility expands, due for instance to a steep drop in the value of the Japanese yen, active responses to minimize any negative impacts on exports and investor confidence should be taken as necessary -- by way, for instance, of smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and measures to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  soundness," Mr. Kim said at a news conference.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  refers to its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as "smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to slow sharp won moves.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Japan's vice finance minister Takehiko Nakao told The Wall Street Journal Monday the sliding yen is a mere "side effect" of Mr. Abe's efforts to stimulate Japan's economy, and reflects changed economic fundamentals at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 But a top Japanese economic policymaker warned that further declines could actually harm the economy.\par \par \uc2 "The yen has come to a good level," Economy Minister Akira Amari said in an interview on Japan's TBS television station, the latest in a series of comments from Cabinet members suggesting officials are happy with the yen around 90 to the dollar--but not much weaker.\par \par \uc2 "If it falls further to the three-digit level, it would boost import prices, weighing on the everyday life of the nation," Mr. Amari said, implying that officials may try to tap the brakes if markets push the dollar toward the 100 yen level--a mark unseen since 2009. The dollar traded in the 89-yen range in Asia markets Monday, and flirted with the 90-yen level for the first time since June 2010.\par \par \uc2 While a weak yen improves Japan's export competitiveness, a strong yen also has certain benefits, such as lowering the relative cost of goods imported into Japan. That has been particularly important since the March 2011 Fukushima nuclear disaster forced a near-total shutdown of Japan's nuclear power industry, leading to a sharp increase in fuel imports.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Amari's warning about higher import costs echoes remarks from top Japanese executives in recent days.\par \par \uc2 "The weaker yen is good for the broader Japanese economy, but I'm most concerned about energy prices," \uc2 Toshiba Corp\uc2 . (6502.TO) President \uc2 Norio Sasaki\uc2  said at a gathering of business leaders last week. "The key is how to achieve a weaker yen after finding the right balance between energy and the yen's depreciation," he added.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Abe's push for a weaker yen and Seoul's "smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market calls to mind the fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars at the height of the global financial crisis, as governments sought to shield their local companies from the downturn.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Nakao said Tokyo isn't seeking to engage in competitive devaluations. But the simultaneous yen decline and won rise threatens the competitiveness of South Korean exporters, though they still enjoy a large \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  advantage after the won halved in value relative to the yen in 2008, during the global financial crisis. A \uc2 Hyundai Motor Group\uc2  executive recently referred to the won's rise and yen's slide as "a double-torture situation."\par \par \uc2 Also speaking Monday, South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan refused to comment on Japan's efforts to weaken the yen, but noted that the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  leading nations has said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels should be set by the market in line with economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bahk said Seoul could expand its range of so-called macroprudential measures to prevent speculators from driving the won's value.\par \par \uc2 Seoul's Finance Ministry in November tightened curbs on banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward contracts to reduce volatility in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. A senior ministry official said last month the government was considering increasing the frequency of checks on banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward positions, from monthly to weekly or even daily.\par \par \uc2 -- Chester Yung contributed to this article\par \par \uc2 Write to Michael S. Arnold at michael.arnold@dowjones.com [ 01-14-13 0824ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020130114e91e0003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
