\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-South Korea  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  bought 14 tonnes of gold in Nov\b0\par\par\uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2012\par 21:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * \uc2 Bank of Korea\uc2  increases gold reserves to 84.4 tonnes\par \par \uc2  * Gold holdings at 1.2 pct of total foreign reserves, up from 0.9 pct \par \par \uc2 (Adds background, comments, bullet points)\par \par \uc2  By Christine Kim\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Dec 5 (Reuters) - \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it bought 14 tonnes of gold in November using its foreign reserves in order to spread its portfolio risks, while releasing data showing total reserves rose after talk of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2  bought the gold for $780 million, the fourth purchase in about one-and-a-half years and lifting the proportion of gold in its total foreign reserves to 1.2 percent from the previous 0.9 percent, it said in a statement.\par \par \uc2  "Gold is a physical, safe asset and allows (the country) to deal with changes in the international financial environment more effectively," it said in a statement, without providing more details on the purchase.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2  now holds 84.4 tonnes of gold, valued at $3.76 billion in terms of purchase prices, up nearly six-fold from 14.4 tonnes before June last year.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2  made its first gold purchase in more than a decade between June and July last year, joining some central banks in diversifying their increasing foreign reserves away from the U.S. dollar and low-yielding government bonds.\par \par \uc2  Official sector buying has become a key factor supporting gold demand and prices in recent years. \par \par \uc2  "It (South Korea's gold buying) points to stronger support for gold prices from central banks," said Philip Klapwijk, global head of metals analytics at Thomson Reuters GFMS, a metals consultancy.\par \par \uc2  "If private sector investment falters and prices dip, central banks' buying supports prices at higher levels than if this demand were not present. It is a substantial additional source of demand for gold bullion."\par \par \uc2  Central banks around the world bought a total of 351.8 tonnes of gold in the first nine months of 2013, up 2 percent from a year earlier, data from the \uc2 World Gold Council\uc2  showed. \par \par \uc2  In comparison, private sector gold investment demand during the period dropped nearly 8 percent on the year to 1,139.3 tonnes, the data also showed.\par \par \uc2  Spot gold traded just below $1,700 an ounce on Wednesday, up more than 8 percent so far this year. \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2  said it now expected its ranking among central banks around the world in terms of gold holdings to rise to 36th from 40th.\par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said foreign reserves rose by $2.6 billion last month to a record $326.09 billion, extending its record-breaking streak to a fourth consecutive month.\par \par \uc2  It attributed the increase to investment gains but the data came after reports by traders of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by South Korean authorities during the month to curb the won's\par \par \uc2 rapid appreciation.\par \par \uc2  On Nov. 22 alone, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders estimated authorities bought up to $1 billion in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to temper a stronger won, which hurts the competitiveness of South Korean exporters.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials declined to comment on the talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had the world's seventh-largest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as of the end of October, held 91.7 percent of its reserves in the form of securities.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  South Korean foreign reserves (in $ bln, at end-month):  \par Nov      Oct      Sept     Aug      July     June     May\par 326.09   323.46   322.01   316.88   314.35   312.38   310.87\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Rujun Shen in SINGAPORE; Editing by Ed\par Davies)\f28 \par \par \uc2 KOREA-ECONOMY/RESERVES (UPDATE 1) | ABX | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | C | MTL | RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121204e8c400159\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
