\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Korea Intervenes \highlight\uc2  to Curb Won\b0\par\par\uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2012\par 11:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL--\uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2  apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Thursday to brake the won's steep rise, and the authorities warned of further action against "excessive" moves that can hurt the nation's exporters.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves have been excessive," Deputy Finance Minister Choi Jong-ku told a hastily assembled news conference as the won climbed to a more than 14-month high against the dollar. "There are worries that the pace of our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation is relatively too fast."\par \par \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold won for dollars after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to 1,080.20 won, its lowest since early September 2011, according to six traders in Seoul. Late in the day, the BOK sought to drive the dollar higher, buying it near KRW1,085 and bringing the day's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  total above an estimated $1 billion, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The BOK's action boosted the greenback as high as KRW1,086.00. It ended local trading at KRW1,085.90, up from KRW1,083.20 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The authorities appear to be taking some forceful action," one trader said. It was the first time since late October that traders have reported what appeared to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined comment.\par \par \uc2 The won, one of Asia's fastest-rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months, has accelerated its gains since October as Korea has posted big current-account surpluses and as fears over the euro-zone debt crisis and a potential U.S. budget crisis have eased somewhat, prompting investors to buy riskier assets in emerging markets in Asia and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 The Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is up some 9% against the dollar since the end of May and has jumped 14% against the yen, which has fallen on expectations that the Bank of Japan will take more aggressive action to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Not only is the strong won a worry to Korea's export powerhouses, making their goods less competitive overseas, it also invites potentially destabilizing capital flows into Korea. In recent years, especially as very loose monetary policy in the U.S. has driven investors overseas in a hunt for higher yields, Seoul has repeatedly sought to limit the overseas \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure of its banks and companies.\par \par \uc2 "If we continue to let the won rise as fast as now, we could see speculative capital flow in at a much faster pace," Mr. Choi said. "We need to ease or contain this."\par \par \uc2 Still, despite the won's recent sharp gains, some market participants don't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and jawboning will have a lasting impact, as Korea's robust fundamentals argue for further gains.\par \par \uc2 "The won is still undervalued against the dollar and clearly so against the yen," said Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. With its recent gains, the won is nearing fair value against the dollar but still has a long way to rise against the yen, she said.\par \par \uc2 A trader at a foreign bank said a rebound by the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to KRW1,087-KRW1,088 could be "a very good chance to offload dollars."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Choi's comments Thursday reflected stepped-up concern from the authorities. Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan had warned Wednesday that the government would closely monitor the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and take appropriate measures if needed.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Choi said the government will likely decide next week whether to tighten curbs on banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward contracts as a way to reduce the volatility in the local market. The BOK and the Financial Supervisory Service in recent weeks have jointly investigated what role banks have played in the won's surge. Investigators have completed on-spot inspections and are analyzing the data in order to decide next week whether to take action, Mr. Choi said.\par \par \uc2 Further restricting won forward positions is Seoul's "first option," he said. Those restrictions were tightened in July 2011 as capital inflows intensified, sending the won to highs it is now revisiting.\par \par \uc2 Korean banks can hold forward positions worth 40% of their capital, while foreign banks operating in Korea must limit forward positions to 200% of capital. Local media have reported that the government may cut those limits to 30% for local banks and 150% for foreign lenders.\par \par \uc2 Write to Kwanwoo Jun at kwanwoo.jun@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 22, 2012 05:25 ET (10:25 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121122e8bm0032l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
