\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/Korea Real Time: South Korea: On  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , We're Not Hong Kong\b0\par\par\uc2 555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2012\par 12:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Korea Real Time Report blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/korearealtime"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/korearealtime }}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Kwanwoo Jun\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  is shrugging off speculation that it might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow recent gains in the won after Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to damp its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.\par \par \uc2 The won has risen more than 4% against the U.S. dollar this year, hitting a 13-month high last Friday after seven straight days of gains.\par \par \uc2 The concern for Seoul is that the strong won is a weight on local exporters. Most Korean newspapers last week quoted traders and analysts who said they were closely watching whether authorities would step into the market as the exchange rate approached the psychologically important level of 1,100 won per dollar. Some speculated the government might move to tighten existing curbs on capital flows.\par \par \uc2 Recent stimulus measures by the U.S. Federal Reserve Board and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have led money managers to pump more liquidity into emerging markets, including South Korea and Hong Kong in Asia.\par \par \uc2 In response, some central banks in the region have sought to stop their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening further, to help keep their exports competitively priced.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Saturday said it had bought US$603 million to prevent the Hong Kong dollar from rising too much. The Bank of Japan has recently threatened to slow the yen's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 So far South Korean authorities have not acted visibly to curb the won's rally, but some traders suspect they've \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  stealthily through their agent banks or by mobilizing state-linked corporations to invest in money markets.\par \par \uc2 After hitting a high of 1,103.30 on Friday, the dollar traded in a band of 1,103-1,106 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Asked Friday if he was concerned about the won's recent rise, Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan gave an oblique response.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not supposed to comment on the pace of the won's rise," Mr. Bahk told reporters on the sidelines of a conference.\par \par \uc2 But he added: "You may want to look at the average base exchange rate, on which the budget was drawn up a year ago."\par \par \uc2 Ministry officials later confirmed the budgeted rate for 2012 was 1,070 won per dollar -- still above the current level. It may be a leap to assume that 's a level Seoul wants to defend, but it's clear Mr. Bahk keeps it in mind.\par \par \uc2 For next year's budget, the Finance Ministry has set the average base exchange rate at 1,130. Ministry officials cautioned that rate is relevant for dollar-based buying of military equipment or crude oil, for example, but doesn't necessarily bind monetary-policy makers.\par \par \uc2 "If the won keeps rising next year, it would be a big concern for Korean exporters, but not for us because we could pay less in U.S. dollars" for government purchases overseas, said one Finance Ministry official responsible for budget policy, who asked not to be named. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 22, 2012 06:29 ET (10:29 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121022e8am0022v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
