\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 South Korea \highlight\uc2  Won At 7-Month Low Despite Suspected  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2012\par 10:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Close     Change \par  USD/KRW            1,185.50  +5.00 \par  JPY/KRW            14.8745   +0.0254 \par  3-Year Treasurys   3.35%     unchanged \par  5-Year Treasurys   3.47%     unchanged \par  10-Year Treasurys  3.73%     unchanged \par  20-Year Treasurys  3.83%     unchanged \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won fell for a third straight day against the U.S. dollar Friday, hitting its lowest level in more than seven months despite suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by local authorities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar was weaker in the morning, taking a breather after its recent gains, but strong demand by importers and investment trust firms lifted it later in the session, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose as high as KRW1,185.60 during the session, its highest level since it was at KRW1,192.60 on Oct. 6, 2011.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea was believed to have sold dollars at levels above KRW1,180 to curb the won's losses, according to five Seoul-based traders. But the traders said the BOK selling was too insignificant to reverse the dollar strength.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on May 21, when the dollar rose above KRW1,170.\par \par \uc2 An official at the BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market team declined to comment on the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Amid fears about Greece's potential exit from the euro zone, the risk-sensitive won has lost 4.7% against the dollar so far this month. Market participants said as long as the Greek political conundrum isn't resolved, the won's weak trend is likely to continue, probably until Greece holds a parliamentary election in mid-June.\par \par \uc2 "The market is losing faith in the euro zone and Greece. The dollar is inching closer to the critical KRW1,200 level. Caution will likely heighten over potentially stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities," said a senior dealer at a foreign bank in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 He forecast resistance for the dollar initially at KRW1,190 and then at KRW1,200.\par \par \uc2 Korean government bonds were flat amid a lack of cues.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said uncertainty related to Greece may continue to support safe-haven local bonds, but they are likely to trade in a narrow range for a while.\par \par \uc2 "Most investors have also entered a wait-and-see mode, before key domestic monthly data" including consumer price index and industrial output numbers due next week, said Kim Nam-hyun, a fixed-income analyst at Eugene Futures.\par \par \uc2 He forecast June bond futures to move in a narrow 104.45-104.60 band for the near term.\par \par \uc2 June bond futures rose one tick to 104.55.\par \par \uc2 South Korean financial markets will close Monday for a public holiday and resume trading Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 -By Jieun Shin, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-3700-1905; jieun.shin@dowjones.com [ 25-05-12 0805GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1063 1858   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120525e85p000ey\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
